window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"e2decd80f5595fb11973c532093740b301ae08e052b6784dfd8b59bcee60fb1b":{"markdown":"## 10. Exercises with complete solutions\n\n**Exercise 1.** Explain why changing an implementer by a central element preserves the derivation. For a star derivation prove that any selfadjoint implementer can be shifted to a positive one. Explain which normalization selects the implementer in Theorem 1.\n\n**Solution.** $[k+z,x]=[k,x]$ when $z$ is central. Conversely equality of the two commutators for every $x$ means their difference is central. If $a=a^*$ implements $d=i[a,\\cdot]$, continuous functional calculus gives $a+\\|a\\|1\\geq0$, with the same commutator. Positivity alone permits further positive central shifts. The corner conditions $\\|bz\\|=\\|d|_{Mz}\\|$ for every central projection, together with positivity, give uniqueness by Section 7. The simpler global condition alone need not calibrate every central corner.\n\n**Exercise 2.** In the matrix example calculate $S_3$, $S_5$ and $p(3)$. Check the transfer inequality (28) for $x=E_{32}$, $r=3$, $s=2$.\n\n**Solution.** Equation (34) gives $S_3=\\operatorname{span}\\{E_{31},E_{32}\\}$ and $S_5=\\operatorname{span}\\{E_{31}\\}$. Their range is the third coordinate, so $p(3)=E_{11}+E_{22}$. At the exact threshold $5$, $p(5)=E_{11}+E_{22}$; at $2$, $p(2)=E_{11}$. Thus $p(5)E_{32}(1-p(2))=0$, since the left projection kills the third row. Replacing the frequency $3$ of $E_{32}$ by $-3$ would give the wrong row and violate the sign convention.\n\n**Exercise 3.** Let $M=\\prod_{i\\in I}M_3(\\mathbb C)$ for an arbitrary index set and choose numbers $a_i\\geq0$. On each factor use $h_i=\\operatorname{diag}(0,2a_i,5a_i)$. When does the coordinate commutator define a bounded derivation of the whole product? What are its norm and a centered implementer?\n\n**Solution.** If $A=\\sup_i a_i<\\infty$, the family $h=(h_i)$ belongs to $M$ and gives the derivation. The matrix calculation in each corner and the product norm give $\\|d\\|=5A$. The centered family $\\operatorname{diag}(-5a_i/2,-a_i/2,5a_i/2)$ has norm $5A/2$. If the $a_i$ are unbounded, the family with coordinate $E_{31}$ in every factor has norm one but its purported derivative has coordinate norm $5a_i$, and is not an element of $M$. Thus this coordinate prescription is not an everywhere-defined derivation $M\\to M$. No countability is used in either argument.\n\n**Exercise 4.** On $\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)$ let the diagonal unbounded operator have entries $n$. What goes wrong with applying automatic boundedness to its commutator on the finite matrix span?\n\n**Solution.** On that span the commutator is well defined and sends $E_{jk}$ to $(j-k)E_{jk}$, so its norm on norm-one matrix units is unbounded. The span is not complete in the C*-norm and is a proper subalgebra of $B(\\ell^2)$, whereas L34 assumes an everywhere-defined derivation of a complete C*-algebra. The diagonal unitaries $e^{itn}$ do act on the whole Hilbert space, but their adjoint action is not operator-norm continuous at zero: at $t_m=\\pi/m$, its value on $E_{m+1,1}$ is the negative of that matrix unit, so the action's distance from identity is at least $2$ although $t_m\\to0$. There is consequently no bounded generator on all of $B(\\ell^2)$ to which Theorem 8 could apply.\n\n**Exercise 5.** Show that the dyadic reconstruction in Lemma 3 can be passed through any isometric action filter, and explain why mere pointwise frequency convergence would not suffice.\n\n**Solution.** For the decomposition (10), the operator norm of each filter is at most the $L^1$ norm of its inverse transform, by (6) and isometry. Estimate (11) makes their operator series absolutely convergent. The $L^1$ equality of the inverse transforms identifies its sum with $T_F$, again by (6). Pointwise convergence of the frequency symbols by itself gives no bound on these time-domain $L^1$ norms and therefore no justified convergence of the operator filters. Flatness at the endpoint is precisely what supplied the summable derivative/Plancherel estimate.","html":"<h2>10. Exercises with complete solutions<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise 1.<\/strong> Explain why changing an implementer by a central element preserves the derivation. For a star derivation prove that any selfadjoint implementer can be shifted to a positive one. Explain which normalization selects the implementer in Theorem 1.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([k+z,x]=[k,x]\\)\">\\([k+z,x]=[k,x]\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> is central. Conversely equality of the two commutators for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> means their difference is central. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=a^*\\)\">\\(a=a^*\\)<\/span> implements <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d=i[a,\\cdot]\\)\">\\(d=i[a,\\cdot]\\)<\/span>, continuous functional calculus gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a+\\|a\\|1\\geq0\\)\">\\(a+\\|a\\|1\\geq0\\)<\/span>, with the same commutator. Positivity alone permits further positive central shifts. The corner conditions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|bz\\|=\\|d|_{Mz}\\|\\)\">\\(\\|bz\\|=\\|d|_{Mz}\\|\\)<\/span> for every central projection, together with positivity, give uniqueness by Section 7. The simpler global condition alone need not calibrate every central corner.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise 2.<\/strong> In the matrix example calculate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_3\\)\">\\(S_3\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_5\\)\">\\(S_5\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(3)\\)\">\\(p(3)\\)<\/span>. Check the transfer inequality (28) for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=E_{32}\\)\">\\(x=E_{32}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=3\\)\">\\(r=3\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s=2\\)\">\\(s=2\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> Equation (34) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_3=\\operatorname{span}\\{E_{31},E_{32}\\}\\)\">\\(S_3=\\operatorname{span}\\{E_{31},E_{32}\\}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_5=\\operatorname{span}\\{E_{31}\\}\\)\">\\(S_5=\\operatorname{span}\\{E_{31}\\}\\)<\/span>. Their range is the third coordinate, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(3)=E_{11}+E_{22}\\)\">\\(p(3)=E_{11}+E_{22}\\)<\/span>. At the exact threshold <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(5\\)\">\\(5\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(5)=E_{11}+E_{22}\\)\">\\(p(5)=E_{11}+E_{22}\\)<\/span>; at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\)\">\\(2\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(2)=E_{11}\\)\">\\(p(2)=E_{11}\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(5)E_{32}(1-p(2))=0\\)\">\\(p(5)E_{32}(1-p(2))=0\\)<\/span>, since the left projection kills the third row. Replacing the frequency <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(3\\)\">\\(3\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{32}\\)\">\\(E_{32}\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-3\\)\">\\(-3\\)<\/span> would give the wrong row and violate the sign convention.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise 3.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=\\prod_{i\\in I}M_3(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M=\\prod_{i\\in I}M_3(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span> for an arbitrary index set and choose numbers <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_i\\geq0\\)\">\\(a_i\\geq0\\)<\/span>. On each factor use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_i=\\operatorname{diag}(0,2a_i,5a_i)\\)\">\\(h_i=\\operatorname{diag}(0,2a_i,5a_i)\\)<\/span>. When does the coordinate commutator define a bounded derivation of the whole product? What are its norm and a centered implementer?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\sup_i a_i&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(A=\\sup_i a_i&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, the family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=(h_i)\\)\">\\(h=(h_i)\\)<\/span> belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and gives the derivation. The matrix calculation in each corner and the product norm give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|d\\|=5A\\)\">\\(\\|d\\|=5A\\)<\/span>. The centered family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{diag}(-5a_i/2,-a_i/2,5a_i/2)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{diag}(-5a_i/2,-a_i/2,5a_i/2)\\)<\/span> has norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(5A/2\\)\">\\(5A/2\\)<\/span>. If the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_i\\)\">\\(a_i\\)<\/span> are unbounded, the family with coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{31}\\)\">\\(E_{31}\\)<\/span> in every factor has norm one but its purported derivative has coordinate norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(5a_i\\)\">\\(5a_i\\)<\/span>, and is not an element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Thus this coordinate prescription is not an everywhere-defined derivation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\to M\\)\">\\(M\\to M\\)<\/span>. No countability is used in either argument.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise 4.<\/strong> On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)\">\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)<\/span> let the diagonal unbounded operator have entries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>. What goes wrong with applying automatic boundedness to its commutator on the finite matrix span?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> On that span the commutator is well defined and sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{jk}\\)\">\\(E_{jk}\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((j-k)E_{jk}\\)\">\\((j-k)E_{jk}\\)<\/span>, so its norm on norm-one matrix units is unbounded. The span is not complete in the C*-norm and is a proper subalgebra of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span>, whereas L34 assumes an everywhere-defined derivation of a complete C*-algebra. The diagonal unitaries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{itn}\\)\">\\(e^{itn}\\)<\/span> do act on the whole Hilbert space, but their adjoint action is not operator-norm continuous at zero: at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_m=\\pi/m\\)\">\\(t_m=\\pi/m\\)<\/span>, its value on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{m+1,1}\\)\">\\(E_{m+1,1}\\)<\/span> is the negative of that matrix unit, so the action's distance from identity is at least <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\)\">\\(2\\)<\/span> although <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_m\\to0\\)\">\\(t_m\\to0\\)<\/span>. There is consequently no bounded generator on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span> to which Theorem 8 could apply.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise 5.<\/strong> Show that the dyadic reconstruction in Lemma 3 can be passed through any isometric action filter, and explain why mere pointwise frequency convergence would not suffice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> For the decomposition (10), the operator norm of each filter is at most the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1\\)\">\\(L^1\\)<\/span> norm of its inverse transform, by (6) and isometry. Estimate (11) makes their operator series absolutely convergent. The <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1\\)\">\\(L^1\\)<\/span> equality of the inverse transforms identifies its sum with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_F\\)\">\\(T_F\\)<\/span>, again by (6). Pointwise convergence of the frequency symbols by itself gives no bound on these time-domain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1\\)\">\\(L^1\\)<\/span> norms and therefore no justified convergence of the operator filters. Flatness at the endpoint is precisely what supplied the summable derivative/Plancherel estimate.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L35.md","start_offset":40726,"end_offset":44640,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e2cf3485cbcd5705241c53ea09a07506f03690e7ef8d079f70fe83006c320e69":{"markdown":"## FF-4. Closed nonnegative forms, including a nondense finite domain\n\nA nonnegative extended form $q$ on a Hilbert space is a function into $[0,\\infty]$ whose finite domain $D_q$ is linear, and whose restriction there is a nonnegative quadratic form with the usual complex polarization. It is closed when $D_q$, equipped with\n$$\n\\langle v,w\\rangle_q=\\langle v,w\\rangle+q(v,w),\n$$\nis complete. We now prove its spectral representation, allowing $D_q$ not to be dense.\n\nPut $H_0=\\overline{D_q}$, let $V=D_q$ with this graph inner product, and let $j:V\\to H_0$ be inclusion. It is bounded, injective, contractive, and has dense range. Thus $B=jj^*$ is a positive contraction on $H_0$ with zero kernel. Its square root is provided by the proved spectral calculus. On $\\operatorname{Ran}j^*$ the rule\n$$\nj^*u\\longmapsto B^{1/2}u\n$$\nis an isometry, since both squared norms equal $\\langle Bu,u\\rangle$. The first range is dense in $V$, because $(\\operatorname{Ran}j^*)^\\perp=\\ker j=0$; the second is dense in $H_0$, because $B^{1/2}$ has zero kernel. The isometry extends to a unitary $W:V\\to H_0$. Taking adjoints of $Wj^*=B^{1/2}$ gives\n$$\nj=B^{1/2}W.\n\\tag{FF8}\n$$\nThis is a proof of the particular polar factorization needed here, not a polar-decomposition import. It yields\n$$\nD_q=\\operatorname{Ran}B^{1/2},\\qquad\n\\|B^{-1/2}v\\|^2=\\|v\\|^2+q(v).\n$$\nThe exact inverse-range and spectral-domain calculus therefore gives a positive self-adjoint operator $A=B^{-1}-I$ on $H_0$, with\n$$\nD(A^{1/2})=D_q,\\qquad q(v)=\\|A^{1/2}v\\|^2.\n\\tag{FF9}\n$$\nDeclare $q(v)=\\infty$ off this domain, including whenever $v$ has a nonzero component in $H_0^\\perp$. The pair $(A,H_0^\\perp)$ is uniquely determined: the graph Hilbert space and its inclusion determine $B$, and then spectral calculus determines $A$.\n\nConversely every such pair gives a closed form, because the graph norm of the closed operator $A^{1/2}$ is complete. It is lower semicontinuous for the Hilbert norm: its form is the supremum of the continuous quadratic functions of the bounded spectral truncations\n$$\nh_n=\\min(A,n)\\ \\text{ on }H_0,\\qquad h_n=nI\\ \\text{ on }H_0^\\perp.\n\\tag{FF10}\n$$\nIf a form is initially known to be lower semicontinuous, its graph norm is complete. A graph-Cauchy sequence $v_n$ converges in Hilbert norm to $v$; lower semicontinuity applied to $v_n-v_m$, with $m\\to\\infty$, gives $q(v_n-v)$ bounded by the graph-Cauchy tail. It follows that $v\\in D_q$ and $v_n\\to v$ in graph norm. Thus either characterization of closedness is available.\n\nFor the integral form $q(v)=\\int\\|T_t v\\|^2\\,dt$, where each $T_t$ is bounded and the vector functions are measurable, graph completeness follows directly. A graph-Cauchy sequence gives a Hilbert limit $v$ and a vector $L^2$ limit $F$. The a.e. subsequence result of [FF-3](#oa-flow.ff.4) identifies $F(t)=T_tv$, since $T_t v_n\\to T_tv$ for each fixed $t$. This proves completeness and retains the possible infinite part.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.ff.6\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>FF-4. Closed nonnegative forms, including a nondense finite domain<\/h2>\n<p>A nonnegative extended form <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> on a Hilbert space is a function into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,\\infty]\\)\">\\([0,\\infty]\\)<\/span> whose finite domain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_q\\)\">\\(D_q\\)<\/span> is linear, and whose restriction there is a nonnegative quadratic form with the usual complex polarization. It is closed when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_q\\)\">\\(D_q\\)<\/span>, equipped with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\langle v,w\\rangle_q=\\langle v,w\\rangle+q(v,w),\n\\]\">\\[\n\\langle v,w\\rangle_q=\\langle v,w\\rangle+q(v,w),\n\\]<\/span>\nis complete. We now prove its spectral representation, allowing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_q\\)\">\\(D_q\\)<\/span> not to be dense.<\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0=\\overline{D_q}\\)\">\\(H_0=\\overline{D_q}\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V=D_q\\)\">\\(V=D_q\\)<\/span> with this graph inner product, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j:V\\to H_0\\)\">\\(j:V\\to H_0\\)<\/span> be inclusion. It is bounded, injective, contractive, and has dense range. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=jj^*\\)\">\\(B=jj^*\\)<\/span> is a positive contraction on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0\\)\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span> with zero kernel. Its square root is provided by the proved spectral calculus. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ran}j^*\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ran}j^*\\)<\/span> the rule\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nj^*u\\longmapsto B^{1/2}u\n\\]\">\\[\nj^*u\\longmapsto B^{1/2}u\n\\]<\/span>\nis an isometry, since both squared norms equal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle Bu,u\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle Bu,u\\rangle\\)<\/span>. The first range is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\operatorname{Ran}j^*)^\\perp=\\ker j=0\\)\">\\((\\operatorname{Ran}j^*)^\\perp=\\ker j=0\\)<\/span>; the second is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0\\)\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B^{1/2}\\)\">\\(B^{1/2}\\)<\/span> has zero kernel. The isometry extends to a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W:V\\to H_0\\)\">\\(W:V\\to H_0\\)<\/span>. Taking adjoints of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Wj^*=B^{1/2}\\)\">\\(Wj^*=B^{1/2}\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nj=B^{1/2}W.\n\\tag{FF8}\n\\]\">\\[\nj=B^{1/2}W.\n\\tag{FF8}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a proof of the particular polar factorization needed here, not a polar-decomposition import. It yields\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD_q=\\operatorname{Ran}B^{1/2},\\qquad\n\\|B^{-1/2}v\\|^2=\\|v\\|^2+q(v).\n\\]\">\\[\nD_q=\\operatorname{Ran}B^{1/2},\\qquad\n\\|B^{-1/2}v\\|^2=\\|v\\|^2+q(v).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe exact inverse-range and spectral-domain calculus therefore gives a positive self-adjoint operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=B^{-1}-I\\)\">\\(A=B^{-1}-I\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0\\)\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span>, with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(A^{1/2})=D_q,\\qquad q(v)=\\|A^{1/2}v\\|^2.\n\\tag{FF9}\n\\]\">\\[\nD(A^{1/2})=D_q,\\qquad q(v)=\\|A^{1/2}v\\|^2.\n\\tag{FF9}\n\\]<\/span>\nDeclare <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(v)=\\infty\\)\">\\(q(v)=\\infty\\)<\/span> off this domain, including whenever <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> has a nonzero component in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0^\\perp\\)\">\\(H_0^\\perp\\)<\/span>. The pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((A,H_0^\\perp)\\)\">\\((A,H_0^\\perp)\\)<\/span> is uniquely determined: the graph Hilbert space and its inclusion determine <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, and then spectral calculus determines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely every such pair gives a closed form, because the graph norm of the closed operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^{1/2}\\)\">\\(A^{1/2}\\)<\/span> is complete. It is lower semicontinuous for the Hilbert norm: its form is the supremum of the continuous quadratic functions of the bounded spectral truncations\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nh_n=\\min(A,n)\\ \\text{ on }H_0,\\qquad h_n=nI\\ \\text{ on }H_0^\\perp.\n\\tag{FF10}\n\\]\">\\[\nh_n=\\min(A,n)\\ \\text{ on }H_0,\\qquad h_n=nI\\ \\text{ on }H_0^\\perp.\n\\tag{FF10}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf a form is initially known to be lower semicontinuous, its graph norm is complete. A graph-Cauchy sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_n\\)\">\\(v_n\\)<\/span> converges in Hilbert norm to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span>; lower semicontinuity applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_n-v_m\\)\">\\(v_n-v_m\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\to\\infty\\)\">\\(m\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>, gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(v_n-v)\\)\">\\(q(v_n-v)\\)<\/span> bounded by the graph-Cauchy tail. It follows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in D_q\\)\">\\(v\\in D_q\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_n\\to v\\)\">\\(v_n\\to v\\)<\/span> in graph norm. Thus either characterization of closedness is available.<\/p>\n<p>For the integral form <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(v)=\\int\\|T_t v\\|^2\\,dt\\)\">\\(q(v)=\\int\\|T_t v\\|^2\\,dt\\)<\/span>, where each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_t\\)\">\\(T_t\\)<\/span> is bounded and the vector functions are measurable, graph completeness follows directly. A graph-Cauchy sequence gives a Hilbert limit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> and a vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> limit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>. The a.e. subsequence result of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-flow.ff.4\">FF-3<\/a> identifies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(t)=T_tv\\)\">\\(F(t)=T_tv\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_t v_n\\to T_tv\\)\">\\(T_t v_n\\to T_tv\\)<\/span> for each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>. This proves completeness and retains the possible infinite part.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-FF.md","start_offset":16705,"end_offset":19666,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e29f53c996c56d9fba26054a55042335e1013923f4701c129fb7c3bda806ba67":{"markdown":"# The KMS strip and a complete analytic finite-star core\n\n*CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.*\n\nLet \\(\\varphi\\) be a faithful normal semifinite weight on an arbitrary von Neumann algebra \\(M\\). There is no separability hypothesis. Use the linear-first GNS inner product, \\(N=\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\), \\(A=N\\cap N^*\\), the finite linear extension \\(\\varphi_0\\), and the recovered closed operator \\(S=J\\Delta^{1/2}\\), with \\(S\\Lambda(x)=\\Lambda(x^*)\\) on \\(A\\). Write \\(U_t=\\Delta^{it}\\) and \\(\\sigma_t=\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\).\n\nThe actual inputs are [GW-1–5](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1), [WR-3–5](OA-FLOW-WR.md#wr-3), [MW-4](OA-FLOW-MW.md#mw-4), the normal-weight bounded graph and lower semicontinuity in [EW-2–3](OA-FLOW-EW.md#ew-2) with [the required normality hypothesis](OA-FLOW-EW.md#ew-2), [CP01–06](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.1), [SF, SB-0–6 and SF-3–4](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf0), and [FF-1](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.2) with its precisely linked scalar foundations. All uses of unbounded powers below refer to the actual spectral calculus with domains, not a formal complex power rule.\n\nFor free human context see [Hiai, §2.2 and §7.1(C), printed pp.17–19 and 64](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.02383v1). The local proofs below reconstruct the spectral, integral and domain steps; the assertions and omitted details in the notes are not imported as proofs. The first theorem is existence of the KMS strip. Uniqueness of the automorphism group is a separate theorem.\n\nActual earlier proof ranges: [OA-FLOW.GW.1](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1), [OA-FLOW.GW.2](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.2), [OA-FLOW.GW.3](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.3), [OA-FLOW.GW.4](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.4), [OA-FLOW.GW.5](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.5), [OA-FLOW.WR.1](OA-FLOW-WR.md#oa-flow.wr.1), [OA-FLOW.WR.2](OA-FLOW-WR.md#oa-flow.wr.2), [OA-FLOW.WR.3](OA-FLOW-WR.md#oa-flow.wr.3), [OA-FLOW.WR.4](OA-FLOW-WR.md#oa-flow.wr.4), [OA-FLOW.WR.5](OA-FLOW-WR.md#oa-flow.wr.5), [OA-FLOW.MW.4](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4), [OA-FLOW.NF.5](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.5), [OA-FLOW.EW.2](OA-FLOW-EW.md#oa-flow.ew.2), [OA-FLOW.EW.3](OA-FLOW-EW.md#oa-flow.ew.3), [OA-FLOW.EW.5](OA-FLOW-EW.md#oa-flow.ew.5), [OA-FLOW.CP.1](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.1), [OA-FLOW.CP.2](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.2), [OA-FLOW.CP.3](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.3), [OA-FLOW.CP.4](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.4), [OA-FLOW.CP.5](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.5), [OA-FLOW.CP.6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6), [OA-FLOW.SF.SF0](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf0), [OA-FLOW.SF.SB0](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb0), [OA-FLOW.SF.SB1](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb1), [OA-FLOW.SF.SB2](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb2), [OA-FLOW.SF.SB3](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb3), [OA-FLOW.SF.SB4](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb4), [OA-FLOW.SF.SB5](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb5), [OA-FLOW.SF.SB6](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb6), [OA-FLOW.SF.SF3](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf3), [OA-FLOW.SF.SF4](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf4), [OA-FLOW.FF.1](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.1), [OA-FLOW.FF.2](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.2), [OA-FLOW.CF.1](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.1), [OA-FLOW.CF.6](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.6), [OA-FLOW.CF.7](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.7), [OA-FLOW.CF.8](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.8), [OA-FLOW.SC.4](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-04), [OA-FLOW.SC.5](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-05), [OA-FLOW.SC.8](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-08), [OA-FLOW.SC.9](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-09). The spectral domains, continuous-vector integrals, Gaussian scalar interchange and rectangle/circle complex-analysis arguments are complete in those earlier readers.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.kt.1\"><\/a><a id=\"kt-1\"><\/a>\n\n## KT-1. A closed-strip spectral coefficient with exact bounds\n\nFor \\(\\xi,\\eta\\in D(\\Delta^{1/2})\\), put \\(p_n=1_{[1/n,n]}(\\Delta)\\) and\n\\[\nF_n(z)=\\langle \\Delta^{iz}p_n\\xi,p_n\\eta\\rangle.\n\\]\nEvery \\(F_n\\) is entire: on the indicated compact spectral interval the exponential power series and each derivative converge uniformly on compact sets of \\(z\\). The interval increases to the whole positive spectrum, because \\(\\Delta\\) is injective.\n\nWrite \\(z=t-is\\), \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(0\\leq s\\leq1\\). Spectral calculus and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\nF_n(t-is)\n=\\langle U_t\\Delta^{s/2}p_n\\xi,\\Delta^{s/2}p_n\\eta\\rangle.\n\\tag{KT1}\n\\]\nThe functions \\(F_n\\) converge uniformly on the whole closed strip \\(-1\\leq\\operatorname{Im}z\\leq0\\). Indeed, for \\(m\\geq n\\), the difference is the coefficient on \\(p_m-p_n\\), of absolute value at most\n\\[\n\\left(\\int_{\\lambda\\notin[1/n,n]}(1+\\lambda)\\,d\\mu_\\xi(\\lambda)\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int_{\\lambda\\notin[1/n,n]}(1+\\lambda)\\,d\\mu_\\eta(\\lambda)\\right)^{1/2},\n\\tag{KT2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mu_\\xi(B)=\\|1_B(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\). Both finite tails tend to zero by the scalar convergence theorem. The bound follows from \\(\\lambda^s\\leq1+\\lambda\\); orthogonality of the spectral cutoffs removes cross terms.\n\nLet \\(F_{\\xi,\\eta}\\) be this uniform limit. It is continuous on the closed strip and holomorphic inside: uniform convergence on each rectangle passes the zero boundary integral to the limit, and the proved rectangular Morera criterion [SF-4](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf4) applies. Formula (KT1) passes to the limit, including both edges. In particular,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{\\xi,\\eta}(t)&=\\langle U_t\\xi,\\eta\\rangle,\\\\\nF_{\\xi,\\eta}(t-i)&=\\langle U_t\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi,\\Delta^{1/2}\\eta\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{KT3}\n\\]\nNo vector \\(\\Delta\\xi\\) or \\(\\Delta\\eta\\) is required at the lower edge.\n\nThe exact log-convex estimate is\n\\[\n|F_{\\xi,\\eta}(t-is)|\n\\leq\n(\\|\\xi\\|\\|\\eta\\|)^{1-s}\n(\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\eta\\|)^s.\n\\tag{KT4}\n\\]\nTo verify the scalar step without importing interpolation, for nonzero \\(\\xi\\) normalize \\(\\mu_\\xi\\) to a probability and put \\(m=\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|^2/\\|\\xi\\|^2>0\\). Concavity, or direct differentiation, gives \\(r^s\\leq(1-s)+sr\\) for \\(r\\geq0\\), \\(0<s<1\\). Apply this to \\(r=\\lambda/m\\) and integrate:\n\\(\\int\\lambda^s\\,d\\mu_\\xi\\leq\\|\\xi\\|^{2(1-s)}\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|^{2s}\\).\nThe same estimate for \\(\\eta\\) and Cauchy–Schwarz in (KT1) prove (KT4). Zero vectors and the endpoints follow directly. Injectivity of \\(\\Delta\\) handles the possible zero value of \\(m\\).\n\nThe function is uniquely determined by its continuous real-edge values: the difference of two such holomorphic continuous functions, zero on a real interval, extends by zero across that interval by [SF-4](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf4)'s local rectangular argument; the identity theorem makes it zero throughout the strip. This uniqueness does not require a growth assumption beyond the continuity just stated.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.kt.2\"><\/a><a id=\"kt-2\"><\/a>","html":"<h1>The KMS strip and a complete analytic finite-star core<\/h1>\n<p><em>CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> be a faithful normal semifinite weight on an arbitrary von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. There is no separability hypothesis. Use the linear-first GNS inner product, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\)\">\\(N=\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=N\\cap N^*\\)\">\\(A=N\\cap N^*\\)<\/span>, the finite linear extension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi_0\\)\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)<\/span>, and the recovered closed operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S=J\\Delta^{1/2}\\)\">\\(S=J\\Delta^{1/2}\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\Lambda(x)=\\Lambda(x^*)\\)\">\\(S\\Lambda(x)=\\Lambda(x^*)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_t=\\Delta^{it}\\)\">\\(U_t=\\Delta^{it}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t=\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t=\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The actual inputs are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">GW-1–5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#wr-3\">WR-3–5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#mw-4\">MW-4<\/a>, the normal-weight bounded graph and lower semicontinuity in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EW.html#ew-2\">EW-2–3<\/a> with <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EW.html#ew-2\">the required normality hypothesis<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.1\">CP01–06<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf0\">SF, SB-0–6 and SF-3–4<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.2\">FF-1<\/a> with its precisely linked scalar foundations. All uses of unbounded powers below refer to the actual spectral calculus with domains, not a formal complex power rule.<\/p>\n<p>For free human context see <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.02383v1\">Hiai, §2.2 and §7.1(C), printed pp.17–19 and 64<\/a>. The local proofs below reconstruct the spectral, integral and domain steps; the assertions and omitted details in the notes are not imported as proofs. The first theorem is existence of the KMS strip. Uniqueness of the automorphism group is a separate theorem.<\/p>\n<p>Actual earlier proof ranges: <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">OA-FLOW.GW.1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.2\">OA-FLOW.GW.2<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.3\">OA-FLOW.GW.3<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.4\">OA-FLOW.GW.4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.5\">OA-FLOW.GW.5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#oa-flow.wr.1\">OA-FLOW.WR.1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#oa-flow.wr.2\">OA-FLOW.WR.2<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#oa-flow.wr.3\">OA-FLOW.WR.3<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#oa-flow.wr.4\">OA-FLOW.WR.4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#oa-flow.wr.5\">OA-FLOW.WR.5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">OA-FLOW.MW.4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.5\">OA-FLOW.NF.5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EW.html#oa-flow.ew.2\">OA-FLOW.EW.2<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EW.html#oa-flow.ew.3\">OA-FLOW.EW.3<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EW.html#oa-flow.ew.5\">OA-FLOW.EW.5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.1\">OA-FLOW.CP.1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.2\">OA-FLOW.CP.2<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.3\">OA-FLOW.CP.3<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.4\">OA-FLOW.CP.4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.5\">OA-FLOW.CP.5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">OA-FLOW.CP.6<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf0\">OA-FLOW.SF.SF0<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb0\">OA-FLOW.SF.SB0<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb1\">OA-FLOW.SF.SB1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb2\">OA-FLOW.SF.SB2<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb3\">OA-FLOW.SF.SB3<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb4\">OA-FLOW.SF.SB4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb5\">OA-FLOW.SF.SB5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb6\">OA-FLOW.SF.SB6<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf3\">OA-FLOW.SF.SF3<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf4\">OA-FLOW.SF.SF4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.1\">OA-FLOW.FF.1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.2\">OA-FLOW.FF.2<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.1\">OA-FLOW.CF.1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.6\">OA-FLOW.CF.6<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.7\">OA-FLOW.CF.7<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.8\">OA-FLOW.CF.8<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-04\">OA-FLOW.SC.4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-05\">OA-FLOW.SC.5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-08\">OA-FLOW.SC.8<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-09\">OA-FLOW.SC.9<\/a>. The spectral domains, continuous-vector integrals, Gaussian scalar interchange and rectangle/circle complex-analysis arguments are complete in those earlier readers.<\/p>\n<h2>KT-1. A closed-strip spectral coefficient with exact bounds<\/h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in D(\\Delta^{1/2})\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in D(\\Delta^{1/2})\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_n=1_{[1/n,n]}(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(p_n=1_{[1/n,n]}(\\Delta)\\)<\/span> and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nF_n(z)=\\langle \\Delta^{iz}p_n\\xi,p_n\\eta\\rangle.\n\\]\">\\[\nF_n(z)=\\langle \\Delta^{iz}p_n\\xi,p_n\\eta\\rangle.\n\\]<\/span>\nEvery <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_n\\)\">\\(F_n\\)<\/span> is entire: on the indicated compact spectral interval the exponential power series and each derivative converge uniformly on compact sets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>. The interval increases to the whole positive spectrum, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\)<\/span> is injective.<\/p>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=t-is\\)\">\\(z=t-is\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\leq s\\leq1\\)\">\\(0\\leq s\\leq1\\)<\/span>. Spectral calculus and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nF_n(t-is)\n=\\langle U_t\\Delta^{s/2}p_n\\xi,\\Delta^{s/2}p_n\\eta\\rangle.\n\\tag{KT1}\n\\]\">\\[\nF_n(t-is)\n=\\langle U_t\\Delta^{s/2}p_n\\xi,\\Delta^{s/2}p_n\\eta\\rangle.\n\\tag{KT1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe functions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_n\\)\">\\(F_n\\)<\/span> converge uniformly on the whole closed strip <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-1\\leq\\operatorname{Im}z\\leq0\\)\">\\(-1\\leq\\operatorname{Im}z\\leq0\\)<\/span>. Indeed, for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\geq n\\)\">\\(m\\geq n\\)<\/span>, the difference is the coefficient on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_m-p_n\\)\">\\(p_m-p_n\\)<\/span>, of absolute value at most\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\left(\\int_{\\lambda\\notin[1/n,n]}(1+\\lambda)\\,d\\mu_\\xi(\\lambda)\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int_{\\lambda\\notin[1/n,n]}(1+\\lambda)\\,d\\mu_\\eta(\\lambda)\\right)^{1/2},\n\\tag{KT2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\left(\\int_{\\lambda\\notin[1/n,n]}(1+\\lambda)\\,d\\mu_\\xi(\\lambda)\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int_{\\lambda\\notin[1/n,n]}(1+\\lambda)\\,d\\mu_\\eta(\\lambda)\\right)^{1/2},\n\\tag{KT2}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\xi(B)=\\|1_B(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\xi(B)=\\|1_B(\\Delta)\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>. Both finite tails tend to zero by the scalar convergence theorem. The bound follows from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda^s\\leq1+\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda^s\\leq1+\\lambda\\)<\/span>; orthogonality of the spectral cutoffs removes cross terms.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{\\xi,\\eta}\\)\">\\(F_{\\xi,\\eta}\\)<\/span> be this uniform limit. It is continuous on the closed strip and holomorphic inside: uniform convergence on each rectangle passes the zero boundary integral to the limit, and the proved rectangular Morera criterion <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf4\">SF-4<\/a> applies. Formula (KT1) passes to the limit, including both edges. In particular,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{\\xi,\\eta}(t)&amp;=\\langle U_t\\xi,\\eta\\rangle,\\\\\nF_{\\xi,\\eta}(t-i)&amp;=\\langle U_t\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi,\\Delta^{1/2}\\eta\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{KT3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{\\xi,\\eta}(t)&amp;=\\langle U_t\\xi,\\eta\\rangle,\\\\\nF_{\\xi,\\eta}(t-i)&amp;=\\langle U_t\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi,\\Delta^{1/2}\\eta\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{KT3}\n\\]<\/span>\nNo vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\xi\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\xi\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\eta\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\eta\\)<\/span> is required at the lower edge.<\/p>\n<p>The exact log-convex estimate is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n|F_{\\xi,\\eta}(t-is)|\n\\leq\n(\\|\\xi\\|\\|\\eta\\|)^{1-s}\n(\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\eta\\|)^s.\n\\tag{KT4}\n\\]\">\\[\n|F_{\\xi,\\eta}(t-is)|\n\\leq\n(\\|\\xi\\|\\|\\eta\\|)^{1-s}\n(\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\eta\\|)^s.\n\\tag{KT4}\n\\]<\/span>\nTo verify the scalar step without importing interpolation, for nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> normalize <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\xi\\)<\/span> to a probability and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m=\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|^2/\\|\\xi\\|^2&gt;0\\)\">\\(m=\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|^2/\\|\\xi\\|^2&gt;0\\)<\/span>. Concavity, or direct differentiation, gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^s\\leq(1-s)+sr\\)\">\\(r^s\\leq(1-s)+sr\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\geq0\\)\">\\(r\\geq0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\)<\/span>. Apply this to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=\\lambda/m\\)\">\\(r=\\lambda/m\\)<\/span> and integrate:\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int\\lambda^s\\,d\\mu_\\xi\\leq\\|\\xi\\|^{2(1-s)}\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|^{2s}\\)\">\\(\\int\\lambda^s\\,d\\mu_\\xi\\leq\\|\\xi\\|^{2(1-s)}\\|\\Delta^{1/2}\\xi\\|^{2s}\\)<\/span>.\nThe same estimate for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span> and Cauchy–Schwarz in (KT1) prove (KT4). Zero vectors and the endpoints follow directly. Injectivity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\)<\/span> handles the possible zero value of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The function is uniquely determined by its continuous real-edge values: the difference of two such holomorphic continuous functions, zero on a real interval, extends by zero across that interval by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf4\">SF-4<\/a>'s local rectangular argument; the identity theorem makes it zero throughout the strip. This uniqueness does not require a growth assumption beyond the continuity just stated.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-KT.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":6496,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e2867659872fb4ae16012eaa8533a2156f77347733173348532f651f23a9361a":{"markdown":"## GF2. A partition equipped with finite increasing sequences\n\nWe construct pairwise orthogonal modular-fixed projections \\(p_j\\), \\(j\\in J\\), whose strong sum is \\(1\\), each supplied with positive contractions \\(u_{j,n}\\) of finite \\(\\rho\\)-weight such that \\(u_{j,n}\\uparrow p_j\\).\n\nFirst let \\(0\\neq a\\in Q_+\\) with \\(\\rho(a)<\\infty\\). Average its whole real orbit:\n\n<a id=\"equation-gf5\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n h=\\frac1{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-t^2}\\sigma_t(a)\\,dt.        \\tag{GF5}\n\\]\nThis is the weak-star integral of [KT3](OA-FLOW-KT.md#oa-flow.kt.3) and [CP6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6). The positive kernel and the Gaussian normalization in [FF-1](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.2) give \\(0\\leq h\\leq\\|a\\|1\\).\n\nThe average has finite weight. On a bounded interval choose a partition into intervals \\(I_\\ell\\), with tags \\(t_\\ell\\in I_\\ell\\), and form\n\\[\n h_{\\mathcal P}\n =\\sum_\\ell\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_{I_\\ell}e^{-t^2}\\,dt\\right)\n                  \\sigma_{t_\\ell}(a).\n\\]\nBy [MW4](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4), each positive sum has weight at most \\(\\rho(a)\\) and norm at most \\(\\|a\\|\\). For every normal functional, continuity of its scalar orbit makes these sums converge to the integral on a fixed compact interval as the mesh tends to zero. The omitted tails are bounded by \\(\\|f\\|\\|a\\|\\) times the Gaussian tail mass. Thus, as the intervals exhaust the line, the sums converge ultraweakly to \\(h\\). The lower semicontinuity proved in [EW3](OA-FLOW-EW.md#oa-flow.ew.3) gives\n\\[\n \\rho(h)\\leq\\rho(a)<\\infty.\n\\]\nThe same argument applies to every finite positive \\(a\\), including \\(a=0\\). It uses only finite sums and lower semicontinuity, without interchanging a weight and an integral.\n\n**The support sees the whole orbit.** Work in the faithful normal GNS representation from GF1. For each vector \\(\\xi\\), the function\n\\(t\\mapsto\\langle\\sigma_t(a)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\nis continuous and nonnegative. Because the Gaussian is strictly positive everywhere, its integral is zero exactly when this function vanishes for every real \\(t\\): a positive value would persist on an interval and give a positive integral. Positivity of each operator therefore gives\n\\[\n \\ker h=\\bigcap_{t\\in\\mathbb R}\\ker\\sigma_t(a),\\qquad\n p:=s(h)=\\bigvee_{t\\in\\mathbb R}s(\\sigma_t(a)).\n\\]\nIn particular \\(p\\neq0\\), since the join includes \\(s(a)\\). For any real \\(r\\), the normal automorphism \\(\\sigma_r\\) carries support projections to support projections and preserves joins, by the least-upper-bound property. Translation permutes the whole real orbit, so\n\\[\n \\sigma_r(p)\n =\\bigvee_{t\\in\\mathbb R}s(\\sigma_{r+t}(a))=p.\n\\]\nThis proves modular invariance directly for every real parameter.\n\nFinally put\n\n<a id=\"equation-gf6\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n u_n=h(h+n^{-1}1)^{-1},\\qquad n\\geq1,\\qquad u_0=0.               \\tag{GF6}\n\\]\nThe spectral calculus gives \\(0\\leq u_n\\leq p\\), \\(u_n\\uparrow p\\) strongly, and \\(u_n\\leq nh\\), so \\(\\rho(u_n)<\\infty\\). This supplies a finite increasing sequence for \\(p\\), even when \\(\\rho(p)=\\infty\\). If the original \\(a\\) is supported on a modular-fixed projection \\(r\\), every orbit element, its average, and \\(p\\) are supported on \\(r\\).\n\nNow choose by Zorn a maximal family of nonzero mutually orthogonal modular-fixed projections, each having such a finite increasing sequence. The collection is a set of subsets of the projection set of \\(Q\\); a chain has the union as an upper bound, so the stated choice argument applies. Let \\(p\\) be their join and \\(r=1-p\\). Normality of the automorphisms makes \\(r\\) modular fixed. If \\(r\\neq0\\), [GW4](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.4) gives finite positive contractions \\(w_\\alpha\\uparrow1\\). Consequently \\(r w_\\alpha r\\to r\\) strongly, so at least one such compression \\(a=r w_\\alpha r\\) is nonzero. It has finite weight by ([GF4](OA-FLOW-WS.md#equation-gf4)). All its modular translates are supported on \\(r\\). The construction ([GF5](OA-FLOW-WS.md#equation-gf5))–([GF6](OA-FLOW-WS.md#equation-gf6)) produces a nonzero modular-fixed projection \\(p'\\leq r\\) with the required sequence, contradicting maximality. Therefore\n\n<a id=\"equation-gf7\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\sum_{j\\in J}p_j=1\\quad\\text{strongly}.                         \\tag{GF7}\n\\]\nFor orthogonal projections this means that their finite sums increase strongly to the projection onto the closed span of their ranges; the preceding join is that projection. Choice selects one corresponding sequence \\(u_{j,n}\\) for each \\(p_j\\).\n\nPut\n\n<a id=\"equation-gf8\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n d_{j,n}=u_{j,n}-u_{j,n-1}\\quad(j\\in J,\\ n\\geq1).                \\tag{GF8}\n\\]\nThese are positive, of finite weight since \\(d_{j,n}\\leq u_{j,n}\\), and supported on \\(p_j\\). For every finite \\(F\\subset J\\times\\mathbb N\\),\n\n<a id=\"equation-gf9\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n D_F=\\sum_{(j,n)\\in F}d_{j,n}\\leq1,\\qquad D_F\\longrightarrow1\n       \\text{ strongly}.                                      \\tag{GF9}\n\\]\nFor the bound, within any fixed \\(j\\) a finite subsum is bounded by a full initial telescoping sum \\(u_{j,N}\\leq p_j\\), and the \\(p_j\\)'s are orthogonal. For the limit, the increasing supremum dominates every \\(u_{j,n}\\), hence every \\(p_j\\); ([GF7](OA-FLOW-WS.md#equation-gf7)) forces it to be \\(1\\). The bounded monotone lemma gives the asserted strong limit. This proves an operator partition by finite positive elements, not yet the needed graph approximation.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.weight-sum.gf3\"><\/a><a id=\"oa-flow.gf.3\"><\/a><a id=\"gf-3\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>GF2. A partition equipped with finite increasing sequences<\/h2>\n<p>We construct pairwise orthogonal modular-fixed projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_j\\)\">\\(p_j\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\in J\\)\">\\(j\\in J\\)<\/span>, whose strong sum is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>, each supplied with positive contractions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_{j,n}\\)\">\\(u_{j,n}\\)<\/span> of finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span>-weight such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_{j,n}\\uparrow p_j\\)\">\\(u_{j,n}\\uparrow p_j\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>First let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\neq a\\in Q_+\\)\">\\(0\\neq a\\in Q_+\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(a)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\rho(a)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. Average its whole real orbit:<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n h=\\frac1{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-t^2}\\sigma_t(a)\\,dt.        \\tag{GF5}\n\\]\">\\[\n h=\\frac1{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-t^2}\\sigma_t(a)\\,dt.        \\tag{GF5}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is the weak-star integral of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-KT.html#oa-flow.kt.3\">KT3<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP6<\/a>. The positive kernel and the Gaussian normalization in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.2\">FF-1<\/a> give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\leq h\\leq\\|a\\|1\\)\">\\(0\\leq h\\leq\\|a\\|1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The average has finite weight. On a bounded interval choose a partition into intervals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I_\\ell\\)\">\\(I_\\ell\\)<\/span>, with tags <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_\\ell\\in I_\\ell\\)\">\\(t_\\ell\\in I_\\ell\\)<\/span>, and form\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n h_{\\mathcal P}\n =\\sum_\\ell\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_{I_\\ell}e^{-t^2}\\,dt\\right)\n                  \\sigma_{t_\\ell}(a).\n\\]\">\\[\n h_{\\mathcal P}\n =\\sum_\\ell\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_{I_\\ell}e^{-t^2}\\,dt\\right)\n                  \\sigma_{t_\\ell}(a).\n\\]<\/span>\nBy <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">MW4<\/a>, each positive sum has weight at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(a)\\)\">\\(\\rho(a)\\)<\/span> and norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|a\\|\\)\">\\(\\|a\\|\\)<\/span>. For every normal functional, continuity of its scalar orbit makes these sums converge to the integral on a fixed compact interval as the mesh tends to zero. The omitted tails are bounded by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|f\\|\\|a\\|\\)\">\\(\\|f\\|\\|a\\|\\)<\/span> times the Gaussian tail mass. Thus, as the intervals exhaust the line, the sums converge ultraweakly to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>. The lower semicontinuity proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EW.html#oa-flow.ew.3\">EW3<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\rho(h)\\leq\\rho(a)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\rho(h)\\leq\\rho(a)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe same argument applies to every finite positive <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>, including <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=0\\)\">\\(a=0\\)<\/span>. It uses only finite sums and lower semicontinuity, without interchanging a weight and an integral.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The support sees the whole orbit.<\/strong> Work in the faithful normal GNS representation from GF1. For each vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>, the function\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto\\langle\\sigma_t(a)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto\\langle\\sigma_t(a)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span>\nis continuous and nonnegative. Because the Gaussian is strictly positive everywhere, its integral is zero exactly when this function vanishes for every real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>: a positive value would persist on an interval and give a positive integral. Positivity of each operator therefore gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\ker h=\\bigcap_{t\\in\\mathbb R}\\ker\\sigma_t(a),\\qquad\n p:=s(h)=\\bigvee_{t\\in\\mathbb R}s(\\sigma_t(a)).\n\\]\">\\[\n \\ker h=\\bigcap_{t\\in\\mathbb R}\\ker\\sigma_t(a),\\qquad\n p:=s(h)=\\bigvee_{t\\in\\mathbb R}s(\\sigma_t(a)).\n\\]<\/span>\nIn particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\neq0\\)\">\\(p\\neq0\\)<\/span>, since the join includes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(a)\\)\">\\(s(a)\\)<\/span>. For any real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, the normal automorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_r\\)\">\\(\\sigma_r\\)<\/span> carries support projections to support projections and preserves joins, by the least-upper-bound property. Translation permutes the whole real orbit, so\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sigma_r(p)\n =\\bigvee_{t\\in\\mathbb R}s(\\sigma_{r+t}(a))=p.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sigma_r(p)\n =\\bigvee_{t\\in\\mathbb R}s(\\sigma_{r+t}(a))=p.\n\\]<\/span>\nThis proves modular invariance directly for every real parameter.<\/p>\n<p>Finally put<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n u_n=h(h+n^{-1}1)^{-1},\\qquad n\\geq1,\\qquad u_0=0.               \\tag{GF6}\n\\]\">\\[\n u_n=h(h+n^{-1}1)^{-1},\\qquad n\\geq1,\\qquad u_0=0.               \\tag{GF6}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe spectral calculus gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\leq u_n\\leq p\\)\">\\(0\\leq u_n\\leq p\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_n\\uparrow p\\)\">\\(u_n\\uparrow p\\)<\/span> strongly, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_n\\leq nh\\)\">\\(u_n\\leq nh\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(u_n)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\rho(u_n)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. This supplies a finite increasing sequence for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, even when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(p)=\\infty\\)\">\\(\\rho(p)=\\infty\\)<\/span>. If the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> is supported on a modular-fixed projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, every orbit element, its average, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> are supported on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Now choose by Zorn a maximal family of nonzero mutually orthogonal modular-fixed projections, each having such a finite increasing sequence. The collection is a set of subsets of the projection set of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>; a chain has the union as an upper bound, so the stated choice argument applies. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> be their join and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=1-p\\)\">\\(r=1-p\\)<\/span>. Normality of the automorphisms makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> modular fixed. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\neq0\\)\">\\(r\\neq0\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.4\">GW4<\/a> gives finite positive contractions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_\\alpha\\uparrow1\\)\">\\(w_\\alpha\\uparrow1\\)<\/span>. Consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r w_\\alpha r\\to r\\)\">\\(r w_\\alpha r\\to r\\)<\/span> strongly, so at least one such compression <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=r w_\\alpha r\\)\">\\(a=r w_\\alpha r\\)<\/span> is nonzero. It has finite weight by (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WS.html#equation-gf4\">GF4<\/a>). All its modular translates are supported on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>. The construction (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WS.html#equation-gf5\">GF5<\/a>)–(<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WS.html#equation-gf6\">GF6<\/a>) produces a nonzero modular-fixed projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;\\leq r\\)\">\\(p&#x27;\\leq r\\)<\/span> with the required sequence, contradicting maximality. Therefore<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sum_{j\\in J}p_j=1\\quad\\text{strongly}.                         \\tag{GF7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sum_{j\\in J}p_j=1\\quad\\text{strongly}.                         \\tag{GF7}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor orthogonal projections this means that their finite sums increase strongly to the projection onto the closed span of their ranges; the preceding join is that projection. Choice selects one corresponding sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_{j,n}\\)\">\\(u_{j,n}\\)<\/span> for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_j\\)\">\\(p_j\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Put<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d_{j,n}=u_{j,n}-u_{j,n-1}\\quad(j\\in J,\\ n\\geq1).                \\tag{GF8}\n\\]\">\\[\n d_{j,n}=u_{j,n}-u_{j,n-1}\\quad(j\\in J,\\ n\\geq1).                \\tag{GF8}\n\\]<\/span>\nThese are positive, of finite weight since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_{j,n}\\leq u_{j,n}\\)\">\\(d_{j,n}\\leq u_{j,n}\\)<\/span>, and supported on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_j\\)\">\\(p_j\\)<\/span>. For every finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\subset J\\times\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(F\\subset J\\times\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n D_F=\\sum_{(j,n)\\in F}d_{j,n}\\leq1,\\qquad D_F\\longrightarrow1\n       \\text{ strongly}.                                      \\tag{GF9}\n\\]\">\\[\n D_F=\\sum_{(j,n)\\in F}d_{j,n}\\leq1,\\qquad D_F\\longrightarrow1\n       \\text{ strongly}.                                      \\tag{GF9}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor the bound, within any fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> a finite subsum is bounded by a full initial telescoping sum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_{j,N}\\leq p_j\\)\">\\(u_{j,N}\\leq p_j\\)<\/span>, and the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_j\\)\">\\(p_j\\)<\/span>'s are orthogonal. For the limit, the increasing supremum dominates every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_{j,n}\\)\">\\(u_{j,n}\\)<\/span>, hence every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_j\\)\">\\(p_j\\)<\/span>; (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WS.html#equation-gf7\">GF7<\/a>) forces it to be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. The bounded monotone lemma gives the asserted strong limit. This proves an operator partition by finite positive elements, not yet the needed graph approximation.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-WS.md","start_offset":20344,"end_offset":25718,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e2d10174c943719af2596ffb60c599b8ce4b52246bf92d1ba087d0b3566e4b17":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l125-inputs\"><\/a>\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l125.setting\"><\/a>\n## Full hypotheses and earlier proofs\n\nThroughout, \\(M\\ne0\\) is a concrete von Neumann algebra on an arbitrary complex Hilbert space. The center is \\(Z(M)\\). A group action \\(\\alpha:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\) uses multiplicative notation. The central order results impose no topology, commutativity or amenability on \\(G\\). The separate general noncentral cocycle theorem requires \\(G\\) abelian. When continuity is mentioned it means point-ultraweak continuity, and cocycle continuity uses the ultraweak topology.\n\nFor a unitary \\(v\\), the notation \\(S=\\operatorname{Ad}(v)\\) denotes a bounded linear isometry of the Banach space \\(M\\), with isometric inverse. Its \\(\\operatorname{Sp}(S)\\) is the ordinary Banach-operator spectrum; \\(\\operatorname{Sp}_M(v)\\) is the spectrum of the unitary in its algebra. These are not action spectra. We use \\(V(r)=\\{e^{it}:|t|<r\\}\\), with angular distance on the circle, and the principal argument with values in \\((-\\pi,\\pi)\\) wherever its branch is defined.\n\nThe exact complete earlier inputs are [CF1 compactness, choice and norm series](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.1), [CF2 Banach inverses and resolvent continuity](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.2), [CF4 product compactness](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.4), [CF6 continuous calculus](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.6), [CF7 positive order](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.7), [CF8 arbitrary Hilbert operators](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.8), [SF0 commutant-unitary test](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf0), [SB3 complete unitary Borel calculus](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb3), [CP6 concrete predual](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6), [NF6 normal positive maps](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.6), [PC1 corners and arbitrary joins](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc1), and [PC2 central supports](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc2).\n\nSB3 constructs the unitary calculus for an arbitrary cyclic family. PC1–2 apply without restrictions on dimension or cardinality.","html":"<h2>Full hypotheses and earlier proofs<\/h2>\n<p>Throughout, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\ne0\\)\">\\(M\\ne0\\)<\/span> is a concrete von Neumann algebra on an arbitrary complex Hilbert space. The center is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(M)\\)\">\\(Z(M)\\)<\/span>. A group action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\)\">\\(\\alpha:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\)<\/span> uses multiplicative notation. The central order results impose no topology, commutativity or amenability on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>. The separate general noncentral cocycle theorem requires <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> abelian. When continuity is mentioned it means point-ultraweak continuity, and cocycle continuity uses the ultraweak topology.<\/p>\n<p>For a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span>, the notation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S=\\operatorname{Ad}(v)\\)\">\\(S=\\operatorname{Ad}(v)\\)<\/span> denotes a bounded linear isometry of the Banach space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, with isometric inverse. Its <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Sp}(S)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Sp}(S)\\)<\/span> is the ordinary Banach-operator spectrum; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_M(v)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_M(v)\\)<\/span> is the spectrum of the unitary in its algebra. These are not action spectra. We use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(r)=\\{e^{it}:|t|&lt;r\\}\\)\">\\(V(r)=\\{e^{it}:|t|&lt;r\\}\\)<\/span>, with angular distance on the circle, and the principal argument with values in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((-\\pi,\\pi)\\)\">\\((-\\pi,\\pi)\\)<\/span> wherever its branch is defined.<\/p>\n<p>The exact complete earlier inputs are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.1\">CF1 compactness, choice and norm series<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.2\">CF2 Banach inverses and resolvent continuity<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.4\">CF4 product compactness<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.6\">CF6 continuous calculus<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.7\">CF7 positive order<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.8\">CF8 arbitrary Hilbert operators<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf0\">SF0 commutant-unitary test<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb3\">SB3 complete unitary Borel calculus<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP6 concrete predual<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.6\">NF6 normal positive maps<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc1\">PC1 corners and arbitrary joins<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc2\">PC2 central supports<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>SB3 constructs the unitary calculus for an arbitrary cyclic family. PC1–2 apply without restrictions on dimension or cardinality.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L125.md","start_offset":704,"end_offset":2675,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e2384f8de4fbc219fcfcbe217977cc80af1afc28a53912a600c065006fc84180":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.centerg.exercises\"><\/a>\n## Two null-set checks\n\n**Problem 1.** Explain why a countable dense subgroup suffices in the covariance and fixed-centre proofs, even if \\(G\\) has no countable neighbourhood base.\n\n**Solution.** A countable dense subset generates a countable dense subgroup \\(Q\\). For a continuous map \\(V_y\\), every neighbourhood of \\(V_y(g)\\) pulls back to a neighbourhood of \\(g\\), so it meets \\(Q\\). Therefore \\(V_y(g)\\) lies in the strong closure of \\(V_y(Q)\\). On a separable Hilbert space the strong topology on its unitary group is metrizable, for example by summing the truncated distances on a countable dense vector family. Hence one may approximate \\(V_y(g)\\) by a sequence of \\(V_y(q_n)\\), without obtaining or needing a sequence \\(q_n\\to g\\) in \\(G\\). Strong convergence of unitaries to a unitary implies strong convergence of their adjoints: \\(\\|u_n^*\\xi-u^*\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi-u_nu^*\\xi\\|\\to0\\). Normalizer equalities and commutation equations consequently pass to the limit.\n\n**Problem 2.** A jointly measurable field \\(u(g,y)\\) obeys \\(u(gh,y)=u(g,y)u(h,y)\\) for almost every \\(y\\), for every fixed pair \\((g,h)\\). Why does this not yet give fibre representations? Why can one not obtain the adjoint relation by substituting \\(h=g^{-1}\\) into a product-almost-everywhere law?\n\n**Solution.** The exceptional set may depend on the pair, and an uncountable union of null sets need not be null. Product measurability and sigma-finite Haar measure first yield the law for Haar-almost every pair on almost every fibre. The almost-representation lemma then supplies the missing pointwise representation. A product-null exceptional set can contain the entire inversion graph: in \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) the line \\(h=-g\\) has product Lebesgue measure zero. The involution proof therefore uses nonsingular changes of variables in two independent Haar variables and Fubini, as carried out in the lemma, rather than a substitution on that graph.\n\n**Reading.** M. Takesaki, *Theory of Operator Algebras II*, §X.3, especially Corollary X.3.14, Lemma X.3.15 and Definition X.3.16, pp. 288–289, treats decomposition over the fixed centre. For the scalar-weakly-measurable almost-representation step, keep the separable Hilbert-space hypothesis used here; (N1)–(N2) show why the unrestricted formulation needs an additional hypothesis. All mathematical inputs for this lesson are proved above or linked to their programme proofs at the point of use.","html":"<h2>Two null-set checks<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Problem 1.<\/strong> Explain why a countable dense subgroup suffices in the covariance and fixed-centre proofs, even if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> has no countable neighbourhood base.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> A countable dense subset generates a countable dense subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>. For a continuous map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_y\\)\">\\(V_y\\)<\/span>, every neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_y(g)\\)\">\\(V_y(g)\\)<\/span> pulls back to a neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, so it meets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>. Therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_y(g)\\)\">\\(V_y(g)\\)<\/span> lies in the strong closure of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_y(Q)\\)\">\\(V_y(Q)\\)<\/span>. On a separable Hilbert space the strong topology on its unitary group is metrizable, for example by summing the truncated distances on a countable dense vector family. Hence one may approximate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_y(g)\\)\">\\(V_y(g)\\)<\/span> by a sequence of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_y(q_n)\\)\">\\(V_y(q_n)\\)<\/span>, without obtaining or needing a sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_n\\to g\\)\">\\(q_n\\to g\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>. Strong convergence of unitaries to a unitary implies strong convergence of their adjoints: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|u_n^*\\xi-u^*\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi-u_nu^*\\xi\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|u_n^*\\xi-u^*\\xi\\|=\\|\\xi-u_nu^*\\xi\\|\\to0\\)<\/span>. Normalizer equalities and commutation equations consequently pass to the limit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem 2.<\/strong> A jointly measurable field <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u(g,y)\\)\">\\(u(g,y)\\)<\/span> obeys <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u(gh,y)=u(g,y)u(h,y)\\)\">\\(u(gh,y)=u(g,y)u(h,y)\\)<\/span> for almost every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span>, for every fixed pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((g,h)\\)\">\\((g,h)\\)<\/span>. Why does this not yet give fibre representations? Why can one not obtain the adjoint relation by substituting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=g^{-1}\\)\">\\(h=g^{-1}\\)<\/span> into a product-almost-everywhere law?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The exceptional set may depend on the pair, and an uncountable union of null sets need not be null. Product measurability and sigma-finite Haar measure first yield the law for Haar-almost every pair on almost every fibre. The almost-representation lemma then supplies the missing pointwise representation. A product-null exceptional set can contain the entire inversion graph: in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R^2\\)<\/span> the line <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=-g\\)\">\\(h=-g\\)<\/span> has product Lebesgue measure zero. The involution proof therefore uses nonsingular changes of variables in two independent Haar variables and Fubini, as carried out in the lemma, rather than a substitution on that graph.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading.<\/strong> M. Takesaki, <em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em>, §X.3, especially Corollary X.3.14, Lemma X.3.15 and Definition X.3.16, pp. 288–289, treats decomposition over the fixed centre. For the scalar-weakly-measurable almost-representation step, keep the separable Hilbert-space hypothesis used here; (N1)–(N2) show why the unrestricted formulation needs an additional hypothesis. All mathematical inputs for this lesson are proved above or linked to their programme proofs at the point of use.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L42.md","start_offset":71587,"end_offset":74044,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e2a4b186f9b15a93b948354fd3c108f1f4c4158d286a82766f04175b8263e621":{"markdown":"<a id=\"cgf-9\"><\/a>\n## 9. The modulus and its naturality\n\nLet \\(f:M\\to M'\\) be a normal star isomorphism between algebras in the stated scope. Transport \\(\\psi\\) to \\(f_*\\psi=\\psi\\circ f^{-1}\\). Its support is \\(f(s(\\psi))\\), its support modular group is \\(f\\sigma^\\psi f^{-1}\\), and its centralizer is \\(f(M_\\psi)\\). Transport an implementer \\(w\\) in (CI6) to \\(f(w)\\); this proves preservation of both comparison relations, with inverse transport through \\(f^{-1}\\). Boolean reconstruction therefore gives inverse normal carrier isomorphisms\n\\[\n \\mathcal A(f)(p_M(\\psi))=p_{M'}(f_*\\psi),\\qquad\n \\mathcal A(f)\\Theta_s=\\Theta'_s\\mathcal A(f).\n \\tag{CI40}\n\\]\nThey preserve the original-center embeddings:\n\\(\\mathcal A(f)(\\iota_M(z))=\\iota_{M'}(f(z))\\), first on projection cuts by (CI15), then spectrally. Weight transport proves identity and composition exactly:\n\\((g\\circ f)_*=g_*f_*\\), so \\(\\mathcal A(g\\circ f)=\\mathcal A(g)\\mathcal A(f)\\).\n\nFor chosen trace-scaling presentations and dual weights \\(\\Phi,\\Phi'\\), the weight \\(f_*\\Phi\\) has a supplied continuous eigenfield \\(f(u_{-r})\\) and properly infinite centralizer. DWC compares it with \\(\\Phi'\\) on the whole cone. Thus \\(\\mathcal A(f)(d)=d'\\). Here the supplied continuous field is enough for the comparison. Define\n\\[\n \\operatorname{mod}_{\\Phi,\\Phi'}(f)\n   =(J')^{-1}\\mathcal A(f)|_{\\mathcal A d}J:\n       Z(N)\\longrightarrow Z(N').\n \\tag{CI41}\n\\]\nThis is a normal star isomorphism conjugating \\(\\theta|_{Z(N)}\\) with \\(\\theta'|_{Z(N')}\\). It respects identity, inverses and composition, since (CI40) does and the intermediate \\(J'\\)'s cancel.\n\nFor \\(\\alpha\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\), (CI41) gives a group homomorphism into the normal automorphisms of \\(Z(N)\\) commuting with \\(\\theta\\). If \\(\\alpha=\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\), the weights \\(\\psi\\circ\\alpha^{-1}\\) and \\(\\psi\\) are supported-equivalent via \\(s(\\psi)w^*\\), whose initial projection is \\(w s(\\psi)w^*\\) and final projection is \\(s(\\psi)\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal A(\\alpha)\\) fixes every sigma-finite carrier projection and therefore every projection and every element:\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Inn}(M)\\subseteq\\ker\\operatorname{mod}.\n \\tag{CI42}\n\\]\nChanging the chosen presentation gives the canonical conjugacy (CI41) for the identity of \\(M\\); functoriality proves all choice comparisons. Neither equality of this kernel with inner automorphisms, topological continuity of the modulus, the full characteristic square, nor a factor classification follows from these identities.","html":"<h2>9. The modulus and its naturality<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:M\\to M&#x27;\\)\">\\(f:M\\to M&#x27;\\)<\/span> be a normal star isomorphism between algebras in the stated scope. Transport <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_*\\psi=\\psi\\circ f^{-1}\\)\">\\(f_*\\psi=\\psi\\circ f^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Its support is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(s(\\psi))\\)\">\\(f(s(\\psi))\\)<\/span>, its support modular group is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\sigma^\\psi f^{-1}\\)\">\\(f\\sigma^\\psi f^{-1}\\)<\/span>, and its centralizer is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(M_\\psi)\\)\">\\(f(M_\\psi)\\)<\/span>. Transport an implementer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span> in (CI6) to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(w)\\)\">\\(f(w)\\)<\/span>; this proves preservation of both comparison relations, with inverse transport through <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f^{-1}\\)\">\\(f^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Boolean reconstruction therefore gives inverse normal carrier isomorphisms\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mathcal A(f)(p_M(\\psi))=p_{M&#x27;}(f_*\\psi),\\qquad\n \\mathcal A(f)\\Theta_s=\\Theta&#x27;_s\\mathcal A(f).\n \\tag{CI40}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mathcal A(f)(p_M(\\psi))=p_{M&#x27;}(f_*\\psi),\\qquad\n \\mathcal A(f)\\Theta_s=\\Theta&#x27;_s\\mathcal A(f).\n \\tag{CI40}\n\\]<\/span>\nThey preserve the original-center embeddings:\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A(f)(\\iota_M(z))=\\iota_{M&#x27;}(f(z))\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A(f)(\\iota_M(z))=\\iota_{M&#x27;}(f(z))\\)<\/span>, first on projection cuts by (CI15), then spectrally. Weight transport proves identity and composition exactly:\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((g\\circ f)_*=g_*f_*\\)\">\\((g\\circ f)_*=g_*f_*\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A(g\\circ f)=\\mathcal A(g)\\mathcal A(f)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A(g\\circ f)=\\mathcal A(g)\\mathcal A(f)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For chosen trace-scaling presentations and dual weights <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi,\\Phi&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\Phi,\\Phi&#x27;\\)<\/span>, the weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_*\\Phi\\)\">\\(f_*\\Phi\\)<\/span> has a supplied continuous eigenfield <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(u_{-r})\\)\">\\(f(u_{-r})\\)<\/span> and properly infinite centralizer. DWC compares it with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\Phi&#x27;\\)<\/span> on the whole cone. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A(f)(d)=d&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A(f)(d)=d&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Here the supplied continuous field is enough for the comparison. Define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\operatorname{mod}_{\\Phi,\\Phi&#x27;}(f)\n   =(J&#x27;)^{-1}\\mathcal A(f)|_{\\mathcal A d}J:\n       Z(N)\\longrightarrow Z(N&#x27;).\n \\tag{CI41}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\operatorname{mod}_{\\Phi,\\Phi&#x27;}(f)\n   =(J&#x27;)^{-1}\\mathcal A(f)|_{\\mathcal A d}J:\n       Z(N)\\longrightarrow Z(N&#x27;).\n \\tag{CI41}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a normal star isomorphism conjugating <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta|_{Z(N)}\\)\">\\(\\theta|_{Z(N)}\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta&#x27;|_{Z(N&#x27;)}\\)\">\\(\\theta&#x27;|_{Z(N&#x27;)}\\)<\/span>. It respects identity, inverses and composition, since (CI40) does and the intermediate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J&#x27;\\)\">\\(J&#x27;\\)<\/span>'s cancel.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(M)\\)<\/span>, (CI41) gives a group homomorphism into the normal automorphisms of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(N)\\)\">\\(Z(N)\\)<\/span> commuting with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha=\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\)\">\\(\\alpha=\\operatorname{Ad}(w)\\)<\/span>, the weights <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\circ\\alpha^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\psi\\circ\\alpha^{-1}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span> are supported-equivalent via <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\psi)w^*\\)\">\\(s(\\psi)w^*\\)<\/span>, whose initial projection is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w s(\\psi)w^*\\)\">\\(w s(\\psi)w^*\\)<\/span> and final projection is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\psi)\\)\">\\(s(\\psi)\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A(\\alpha)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A(\\alpha)\\)<\/span> fixes every sigma-finite carrier projection and therefore every projection and every element:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\operatorname{Inn}(M)\\subseteq\\ker\\operatorname{mod}.\n \\tag{CI42}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\operatorname{Inn}(M)\\subseteq\\ker\\operatorname{mod}.\n \\tag{CI42}\n\\]<\/span>\nChanging the chosen presentation gives the canonical conjugacy (CI41) for the identity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>; functoriality proves all choice comparisons. Neither equality of this kernel with inner automorphisms, topological continuity of the modulus, the full characteristic square, nor a factor classification follows from these identities.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CGF.md","start_offset":44461,"end_offset":46949,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e2e06e9ea21e50e65e4fab1e7be5100371cd1f2452fabe77e1c8b208ce6045e7":{"markdown":"<a id=\"cbd-chart\"><\/a>\n## 2. The exact fixed-trace chart of the continuous sector\n\nNow suppose \\(M\\) is a semifinite factor in the stated properly infinite, separable-predual scope, and fix \\(\\tau\\). The [trace-preserving reference construction, TI13–TI17](OA-FLOW-TID.md#tid-3), gives a normal identification\n\\[\n M=P\\overline\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R,dq))\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2),\n \\quad \\tau=\\tau_P\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr},\n \\quad k=1\\otimes e^Q\\otimes1,\\quad \\nu=\\tau_k .\n \\tag{CBD11}\n\\]\nThe trace equality is exact on the whole positive cone: for filling isometries \\(v_i\\), the map \\(x\\otimes e_{ij}\\mapsto v_i x v_j^*\\) is normally implemented by the unitary \\((\\xi_i)\\mapsto\\sum_i v_i\\xi_i\\), and\n\\(\\tau(Y)=\\sum_i\\tau(v_iv_i^*Yv_iv_i^*)\\)\nfollows from the increasing positive sandwiches\n\\(Y^{1/2}\\sum_{i\\in F}v_iv_i^*Y^{1/2}\\).\nThe trace of every scalar rank-one projection is \\(1\\). No scalar trace correction is hidden.\n\nThe full centralizer, its center, and the continuous reference translations are\n\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n N=M_\\nu=P\\overline\\otimes L^\\infty(Q)\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2),\n \\qquad Z(N)=1\\otimes L^\\infty(Q)\\otimes1,\\\\\n u_s=1\\otimes R_{-s}\\otimes1,\\quad\n (R_a\\xi)(q)=\\xi(q-a),\\\\\n u_s g(Q)u_s^*=g(Q+s),\\quad\n \\sigma_t^\\nu(u_s)=e^{-ist}u_s,\\quad\n \\nu\\operatorname{Ad}u_s=e^{-s}\\nu.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{CBD12}\n\\]\nThe last identity follows from the exact cutoff relation\n\\(u_s^*k_\\varepsilon u_s=e^{-s}k_{\\varepsilon e^{-s}}\\)\nand trace cyclicity, so infinite values are included. The unital \\(B(\\ell^2)\\) makes \\(N\\) properly infinite. Its filling rows preserve \\(\\nu\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}\\), as in TI32–TI34. Thus \\(\\nu\\) is faithful dominant, with an actual continuous eigenfield.\n\nThe actual [DS faithful dominant comparison](OA-FLOW-DS.md#ds-5) identifies \\(\\nu\\) with the reference \\(\\Phi\\) used in CGF, by a unitary and whole-cone weight equality. Hence \\(p_M(\\nu)=p_M(\\Phi)=d\\). The continuous-field theorem [DWC2–3](OA-FLOW-DWC.md#dwc-2) is an alternative here, because both reference weights have supplied continuous eigenfields. Define\n\\[\n J_\\tau(g)=\\kappa_\\nu(g(Q)):\n       L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dq)\\overset{\\cong}{\\longrightarrow}\\mathcal A d .\n \\tag{CBD13}\n\\]\nThis is a normal isomorphism of the actual carrier sector. It is precisely CGF's \\(J\\) after identifying \\(Z(N)\\) by its full multiplier algebra.\n\nFor a Borel set \\(D\\), \\(u_s1_D(Q)\\) implements\n\\(e^{-s}\\nu_{1_D(Q)}\\sim\\nu_{1_D(Q+s)}\\),\nwith initial projection \\(1_D(Q)\\) and final projection \\(1_D(Q+s)\\); the equality of weights is (CBD12). Equations (CBD5)–(CBD7) therefore give\n\\[\n \\Theta_s J_\\tau=J_\\tau\\theta_s,\\qquad\n (\\theta_sg)(q)=g(q+s).\n \\tag{CBD14}\n\\]\nIn particular an interval \\(D\\) becomes \\(D-s\\), not \\(D+s\\).\n\nThis chart is independent of the trace-preserving reference construction. If \\(\\nu'=\\tau_{k'}\\) is another such faithful dominant reference, DS supplies a unitary \\(w\\) with\n\\(\\nu=\\nu'\\operatorname{Ad}w\\).\nTrace cyclicity and [TD's whole-cone uniqueness](OA-FLOW-TD.md#td-5) force\n\\[\n k=w^*k'w.\n \\tag{CBD15}\n\\]\nHere trace cyclicity is applied to bounded spectral cutoffs before taking their increasing extended values. Normal spectral transport then gives\n\\(w g(\\log k)w^*=g(\\log k')\\).\nEquation (CBD6) proves\n\\(\\kappa_\\nu(g(\\log k))=\\kappa_{\\nu'}(g(\\log k'))\\).\nThis is equality of the two carrier charts, not merely conjugacy by an undetermined translation.\n\nThe arbitrary semifinite-factor tracial core chart from [CORE9](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-9) remains a separate, compatible description. Its momentum \\(p\\) has flow \\(p\\mapsto p-s\\); reflection \\(q=-p\\) gives (CBD14), paired measure \\(dq/(2\\pi)\\), core trace \\(\\tau\\otimes(e^q\\,dq/(2\\pi))\\), and canonical core density \\(e^{-Q}\\). The positive coordinate evaluated by (CBD1) is \\(e^Q\\). They are different affiliated operators. The reflected core chart exists for finite semifinite factors as well; the CGF construction here assumes properly infinite \\(M\\). A finite factor does not satisfy the main density theorem's properly infinite-centralizer hypothesis. No finite-factor carrier claim is inferred by dropping that hypothesis.","html":"<h2>2. The exact fixed-trace chart of the continuous sector<\/h2>\n<p>Now suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is a semifinite factor in the stated properly infinite, separable-predual scope, and fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span>. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TID.html#tid-3\">trace-preserving reference construction, TI13–TI17<\/a>, gives a normal identification\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n M=P\\overline\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R,dq))\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2),\n \\quad \\tau=\\tau_P\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr},\n \\quad k=1\\otimes e^Q\\otimes1,\\quad \\nu=\\tau_k .\n \\tag{CBD11}\n\\]\">\\[\n M=P\\overline\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R,dq))\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2),\n \\quad \\tau=\\tau_P\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr},\n \\quad k=1\\otimes e^Q\\otimes1,\\quad \\nu=\\tau_k .\n \\tag{CBD11}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe trace equality is exact on the whole positive cone: for filling isometries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_i\\)\">\\(v_i\\)<\/span>, the map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\otimes e_{ij}\\mapsto v_i x v_j^*\\)\">\\(x\\otimes e_{ij}\\mapsto v_i x v_j^*\\)<\/span> is normally implemented by the unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\xi_i)\\mapsto\\sum_i v_i\\xi_i\\)\">\\((\\xi_i)\\mapsto\\sum_i v_i\\xi_i\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau(Y)=\\sum_i\\tau(v_iv_i^*Yv_iv_i^*)\\)\">\\(\\tau(Y)=\\sum_i\\tau(v_iv_i^*Yv_iv_i^*)\\)<\/span>\nfollows from the increasing positive sandwiches\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y^{1/2}\\sum_{i\\in F}v_iv_i^*Y^{1/2}\\)\">\\(Y^{1/2}\\sum_{i\\in F}v_iv_i^*Y^{1/2}\\)<\/span>.\nThe trace of every scalar rank-one projection is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. No scalar trace correction is hidden.<\/p>\n<p>The full centralizer, its center, and the continuous reference translations are\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n N=M_\\nu=P\\overline\\otimes L^\\infty(Q)\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2),\n \\qquad Z(N)=1\\otimes L^\\infty(Q)\\otimes1,\\\\\n u_s=1\\otimes R_{-s}\\otimes1,\\quad\n (R_a\\xi)(q)=\\xi(q-a),\\\\\n u_s g(Q)u_s^*=g(Q+s),\\quad\n \\sigma_t^\\nu(u_s)=e^{-ist}u_s,\\quad\n \\nu\\operatorname{Ad}u_s=e^{-s}\\nu.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{CBD12}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n N=M_\\nu=P\\overline\\otimes L^\\infty(Q)\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2),\n \\qquad Z(N)=1\\otimes L^\\infty(Q)\\otimes1,\\\\\n u_s=1\\otimes R_{-s}\\otimes1,\\quad\n (R_a\\xi)(q)=\\xi(q-a),\\\\\n u_s g(Q)u_s^*=g(Q+s),\\quad\n \\sigma_t^\\nu(u_s)=e^{-ist}u_s,\\quad\n \\nu\\operatorname{Ad}u_s=e^{-s}\\nu.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{CBD12}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last identity follows from the exact cutoff relation\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_s^*k_\\varepsilon u_s=e^{-s}k_{\\varepsilon e^{-s}}\\)\">\\(u_s^*k_\\varepsilon u_s=e^{-s}k_{\\varepsilon e^{-s}}\\)<\/span>\nand trace cyclicity, so infinite values are included. The unital <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span> makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> properly infinite. Its filling rows preserve <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}\\)\">\\(\\nu\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}\\)<\/span>, as in TI32–TI34. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> is faithful dominant, with an actual continuous eigenfield.<\/p>\n<p>The actual <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DS.html#ds-5\">DS faithful dominant comparison<\/a> identifies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> with the reference <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\Phi\\)<\/span> used in CGF, by a unitary and whole-cone weight equality. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_M(\\nu)=p_M(\\Phi)=d\\)\">\\(p_M(\\nu)=p_M(\\Phi)=d\\)<\/span>. The continuous-field theorem <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DWC.html#dwc-2\">DWC2–3<\/a> is an alternative here, because both reference weights have supplied continuous eigenfields. Define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J_\\tau(g)=\\kappa_\\nu(g(Q)):\n       L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dq)\\overset{\\cong}{\\longrightarrow}\\mathcal A d .\n \\tag{CBD13}\n\\]\">\\[\n J_\\tau(g)=\\kappa_\\nu(g(Q)):\n       L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dq)\\overset{\\cong}{\\longrightarrow}\\mathcal A d .\n \\tag{CBD13}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a normal isomorphism of the actual carrier sector. It is precisely CGF's <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> after identifying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(N)\\)\">\\(Z(N)\\)<\/span> by its full multiplier algebra.<\/p>\n<p>For a Borel set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_s1_D(Q)\\)\">\\(u_s1_D(Q)\\)<\/span> implements\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}\\nu_{1_D(Q)}\\sim\\nu_{1_D(Q+s)}\\)\">\\(e^{-s}\\nu_{1_D(Q)}\\sim\\nu_{1_D(Q+s)}\\)<\/span>,\nwith initial projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_D(Q)\\)\">\\(1_D(Q)\\)<\/span> and final projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_D(Q+s)\\)\">\\(1_D(Q+s)\\)<\/span>; the equality of weights is (CBD12). Equations (CBD5)–(CBD7) therefore give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Theta_s J_\\tau=J_\\tau\\theta_s,\\qquad\n (\\theta_sg)(q)=g(q+s).\n \\tag{CBD14}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Theta_s J_\\tau=J_\\tau\\theta_s,\\qquad\n (\\theta_sg)(q)=g(q+s).\n \\tag{CBD14}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn particular an interval <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> becomes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D-s\\)\">\\(D-s\\)<\/span>, not <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D+s\\)\">\\(D+s\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>This chart is independent of the trace-preserving reference construction. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu&#x27;=\\tau_{k&#x27;}\\)\">\\(\\nu&#x27;=\\tau_{k&#x27;}\\)<\/span> is another such faithful dominant reference, DS supplies a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu=\\nu&#x27;\\operatorname{Ad}w\\)\">\\(\\nu=\\nu&#x27;\\operatorname{Ad}w\\)<\/span>.\nTrace cyclicity and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TD.html#td-5\">TD's whole-cone uniqueness<\/a> force\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n k=w^*k&#x27;w.\n \\tag{CBD15}\n\\]\">\\[\n k=w^*k&#x27;w.\n \\tag{CBD15}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere trace cyclicity is applied to bounded spectral cutoffs before taking their increasing extended values. Normal spectral transport then gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w g(\\log k)w^*=g(\\log k&#x27;)\\)\">\\(w g(\\log k)w^*=g(\\log k&#x27;)\\)<\/span>.\nEquation (CBD6) proves\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa_\\nu(g(\\log k))=\\kappa_{\\nu&#x27;}(g(\\log k&#x27;))\\)\">\\(\\kappa_\\nu(g(\\log k))=\\kappa_{\\nu&#x27;}(g(\\log k&#x27;))\\)<\/span>.\nThis is equality of the two carrier charts, not merely conjugacy by an undetermined translation.<\/p>\n<p>The arbitrary semifinite-factor tracial core chart from <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-9\">CORE9<\/a> remains a separate, compatible description. Its momentum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> has flow <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\mapsto p-s\\)\">\\(p\\mapsto p-s\\)<\/span>; reflection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=-p\\)\">\\(q=-p\\)<\/span> gives (CBD14), paired measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dq/(2\\pi)\\)\">\\(dq/(2\\pi)\\)<\/span>, core trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\otimes(e^q\\,dq/(2\\pi))\\)\">\\(\\tau\\otimes(e^q\\,dq/(2\\pi))\\)<\/span>, and canonical core density <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-Q}\\)\">\\(e^{-Q}\\)<\/span>. The positive coordinate evaluated by (CBD1) is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^Q\\)\">\\(e^Q\\)<\/span>. They are different affiliated operators. The reflected core chart exists for finite semifinite factors as well; the CGF construction here assumes properly infinite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. A finite factor does not satisfy the main density theorem's properly infinite-centralizer hypothesis. No finite-factor carrier claim is inferred by dropping that hypothesis.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CBD.md","start_offset":6030,"end_offset":10162,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e25881818de002239b2b8a891936bed835653b462eb970bac01e0ae2875c7aa4":{"markdown":"<a id=\"two-gap-assembly\"><\/a>\n## 12. Assemble the section and its suspension coordinates\n\nApply Section 11 on \\(X_{\\rm sp}\\), retain \\(C_\\infty\\) on the already regular invariant piece, and use (TG30) on the Borel transversal pieces. The pieces are disjoint invariant Borel sets, so the union of their sections is Borel. Each restriction is bi-infinite and \\(m\\)-separated, with all gaps \\(a/b\\). Distinct pieces never meet one orbit. Thus their union \\(C\\) has all the properties in Section 1.\n\nIts successor \\(S:C\\to C\\), predecessor and gap \\(r:C\\to\\{a,b\\}\\) are Borel by Section 2; they are defined at every point. The predecessor is an inverse to \\(S\\), because the section is discrete and bi-infinite in the oriented orbit order.\n\nFor completeness let\n\\[\n D_r=\\{(c,u):c\\in C,\\ 0\\le u<r(c)\\}.\n \\tag{TG33}\n\\]\nThe map \\(D_r\\to X\\), \\((c,u)\\mapsto c+u\\), is Borel and injective, since its half-open intervals partition every orbit. It is onto: the section hits in both directions, so every point has a unique preceding section point. PB gives its Borel inverse. In these coordinates the original flow is exactly the suspension over \\(S\\) with roof \\(r\\), preserving every time and every orbit, with no null reduction. For a positive time, subtract successive roofs until the height lies in its half-open fiber; for a negative time use successive predecessors and add their roofs. Since \\(r\\ge m\\), only finitely many crossings occur on a bounded time interval, and these formulas are Borel on their countably many crossing-count pieces.","html":"<h2>12. Assemble the section and its suspension coordinates<\/h2>\n<p>Apply Section 11 on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_{\\rm sp}\\)\">\\(X_{\\rm sp}\\)<\/span>, retain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_\\infty\\)\">\\(C_\\infty\\)<\/span> on the already regular invariant piece, and use (TG30) on the Borel transversal pieces. The pieces are disjoint invariant Borel sets, so the union of their sections is Borel. Each restriction is bi-infinite and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>-separated, with all gaps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a/b\\)\">\\(a/b\\)<\/span>. Distinct pieces never meet one orbit. Thus their union <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> has all the properties in Section 1.<\/p>\n<p>Its successor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S:C\\to C\\)\">\\(S:C\\to C\\)<\/span>, predecessor and gap <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r:C\\to\\{a,b\\}\\)\">\\(r:C\\to\\{a,b\\}\\)<\/span> are Borel by Section 2; they are defined at every point. The predecessor is an inverse to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>, because the section is discrete and bi-infinite in the oriented orbit order.<\/p>\n<p>For completeness let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n D_r=\\{(c,u):c\\in C,\\ 0\\le u&lt;r(c)\\}.\n \\tag{TG33}\n\\]\">\\[\n D_r=\\{(c,u):c\\in C,\\ 0\\le u&lt;r(c)\\}.\n \\tag{TG33}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_r\\to X\\)\">\\(D_r\\to X\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((c,u)\\mapsto c+u\\)\">\\((c,u)\\mapsto c+u\\)<\/span>, is Borel and injective, since its half-open intervals partition every orbit. It is onto: the section hits in both directions, so every point has a unique preceding section point. PB gives its Borel inverse. In these coordinates the original flow is exactly the suspension over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> with roof <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, preserving every time and every orbit, with no null reduction. For a positive time, subtract successive roofs until the height lies in its half-open fiber; for a negative time use successive predecessors and add their roofs. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\ge m\\)\">\\(r\\ge m\\)<\/span>, only finitely many crossings occur on a bounded time interval, and these formulas are Borel on their countably many crossing-count pieces.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-TG.md","start_offset":53329,"end_offset":54867,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e223fdf3fc230436d99d3767b82eb95d150c2276e1159090423d31a868f73483":{"markdown":"### 4. Supported actions and their implementers\n<a id=\"tic-4\"><\/a><a id=\"oa-flow.tic.4\"><\/a>\n\nA **partial \\(\\alpha\\)-cocycle** is a strong-star continuous family \\(a_s\\in M\\) such that\n\\[\n a_{st}=a_s\\alpha_s(a_t),\\qquad\n a_{s^{-1}}=\\alpha_{s^{-1}}(a_s^*).\n \\tag{TIC12}\n\\]\nThe second identity is part of the definition. Set \\(e_a=a_e\\). The zero family is allowed.\n\n**Proposition 4.** The element \\(e_a\\) is a projection and\n\\[\n a_sa_s^*=e_a,\\qquad a_s^*a_s=\\alpha_s(e_a),\\qquad\n \\alpha_s^a(x)=a_s\\alpha_s(x)a_s^*\\quad(x\\in e_aMe_a)\n \\tag{TIC13}\n\\]\ndefines a normal continuous action on the actual support corner. Its fixed algebra is denoted \\(M^a\\). For a projection \\(q\\in M^a\\), the family \\(a_s^q=q a_s\\) is a partial cocycle with support q.\n\n**Proof.** At the identity (TIC12) gives \\(e_a=e_a^*=e_a^2\\). The product with \\(s^{-1}\\), and then the inverse identity, give \\(a_sa_s^*=e_a\\). Apply the same argument at \\(s^{-1}\\) and transport by \\(\\alpha_s\\) to get \\(a_s^*a_s=\\alpha_s(e_a)\\). Thus each \\(a_s\\) is a partial isometry from \\(\\alpha_s(e_a)H\\) onto \\(e_aH\\). These supports prove multiplicativity, star preservation and preservation of the corner identity in (TIC13). The product law gives \\(\\alpha_s^a\\alpha_t^a=\\alpha_{st}^a\\); the inverse parameter gives the whole inverse map. Normality follows from normality of \\(\\alpha\\) and bounded multiplication. AT's bounded strong-star continuity and the assumed continuity of a prove continuity of this action, hence point-ultraweak continuity by its bounded vector-series test.\n\nFor fixed q, multiply \\(q=a_s\\alpha_s(q)a_s^*\\) by \\(a_s\\) to obtain \\(q a_s=a_s\\alpha_s(q)\\). It follows that \\(q a_s\\) has final projection q and initial projection \\(\\alpha_s(q)\\). The same relation and the product law give its product identity, and at the inverse parameter give its adjoint identity. Its continuity is inherited by multiplication by q. If \\(e_a=0\\), all \\(a_s\\) are zero, the corner and fixed algebra are zero, and every assertion has precisely that interpretation. \\(\\square\\)\n\nFor partial cocycles a,b, define\n\\[\n I(b,a)=\\{x\\in e_bMe_a:x a_s=b_s\\alpha_s(x)\\text{ for every }s\\in G\\}.\n \\tag{TIC14}\n\\]\nThe first index is the target. They are **equivalent**, \\(a\\sim b\\), if a partial isometry \\(w\\in I(b,a)\\) satisfies \\(w^*w=e_a\\) and \\(ww^*=e_b\\). They satisfy \\(a\\precsim b\\) if the same initial equality holds with \\(ww^*\\le e_b\\). These definitions include zero.\n\nThe relation is the exact corner form of the two identities\n\\[\n a_s=w^*b_s\\alpha_s(w),\\qquad b_s=w a_s\\alpha_s(w^*).\n \\tag{TIC15}\n\\]\nIf an element c implements both identities before support compression, their identity-parameter equations are \\(c^*e_bc=e_a\\) and \\(c e_ac^*=e_b\\). Hence \\(w=e_bc e_a\\) has the indicated initial and final projections; compressing the parameter equations gives (TIC15). Conversely the two formulas follow from the intertwiner and support equations, so no choice outside the supports changes the definition. For subequivalence the final projection q is fixed and the second formula is for \\(b^q\\). The map \\(x\\mapsto wxw^*\\), with inverse \\(y\\mapsto w^*yw\\), is a whole normal equivariant isomorphism from the support action of a to the fixed reduction of the support action of b. The inherited whole positive averages and all bounded finite domains therefore transport through it.","html":"<h3>4. Supported actions and their implementers<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>partial <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span>-cocycle<\/strong> is a strong-star continuous family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_s\\in M\\)\">\\(a_s\\in M\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n a_{st}=a_s\\alpha_s(a_t),\\qquad\n a_{s^{-1}}=\\alpha_{s^{-1}}(a_s^*).\n \\tag{TIC12}\n\\]\">\\[\n a_{st}=a_s\\alpha_s(a_t),\\qquad\n a_{s^{-1}}=\\alpha_{s^{-1}}(a_s^*).\n \\tag{TIC12}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe second identity is part of the definition. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_a=a_e\\)\">\\(e_a=a_e\\)<\/span>. The zero family is allowed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 4.<\/strong> The element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_a\\)\">\\(e_a\\)<\/span> is a projection and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n a_sa_s^*=e_a,\\qquad a_s^*a_s=\\alpha_s(e_a),\\qquad\n \\alpha_s^a(x)=a_s\\alpha_s(x)a_s^*\\quad(x\\in e_aMe_a)\n \\tag{TIC13}\n\\]\">\\[\n a_sa_s^*=e_a,\\qquad a_s^*a_s=\\alpha_s(e_a),\\qquad\n \\alpha_s^a(x)=a_s\\alpha_s(x)a_s^*\\quad(x\\in e_aMe_a)\n \\tag{TIC13}\n\\]<\/span>\ndefines a normal continuous action on the actual support corner. Its fixed algebra is denoted <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M^a\\)\">\\(M^a\\)<\/span>. For a projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\in M^a\\)\">\\(q\\in M^a\\)<\/span>, the family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_s^q=q a_s\\)\">\\(a_s^q=q a_s\\)<\/span> is a partial cocycle with support q.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> At the identity (TIC12) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_a=e_a^*=e_a^2\\)\">\\(e_a=e_a^*=e_a^2\\)<\/span>. The product with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s^{-1}\\)\">\\(s^{-1}\\)<\/span>, and then the inverse identity, give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_sa_s^*=e_a\\)\">\\(a_sa_s^*=e_a\\)<\/span>. Apply the same argument at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s^{-1}\\)\">\\(s^{-1}\\)<\/span> and transport by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s\\)<\/span> to get <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_s^*a_s=\\alpha_s(e_a)\\)\">\\(a_s^*a_s=\\alpha_s(e_a)\\)<\/span>. Thus each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_s\\)\">\\(a_s\\)<\/span> is a partial isometry from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s(e_a)H\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s(e_a)H\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_aH\\)\">\\(e_aH\\)<\/span>. These supports prove multiplicativity, star preservation and preservation of the corner identity in (TIC13). The product law gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s^a\\alpha_t^a=\\alpha_{st}^a\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s^a\\alpha_t^a=\\alpha_{st}^a\\)<\/span>; the inverse parameter gives the whole inverse map. Normality follows from normality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> and bounded multiplication. AT's bounded strong-star continuity and the assumed continuity of a prove continuity of this action, hence point-ultraweak continuity by its bounded vector-series test.<\/p>\n<p>For fixed q, multiply <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=a_s\\alpha_s(q)a_s^*\\)\">\\(q=a_s\\alpha_s(q)a_s^*\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_s\\)\">\\(a_s\\)<\/span> to obtain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q a_s=a_s\\alpha_s(q)\\)\">\\(q a_s=a_s\\alpha_s(q)\\)<\/span>. It follows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q a_s\\)\">\\(q a_s\\)<\/span> has final projection q and initial projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s(q)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s(q)\\)<\/span>. The same relation and the product law give its product identity, and at the inverse parameter give its adjoint identity. Its continuity is inherited by multiplication by q. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_a=0\\)\">\\(e_a=0\\)<\/span>, all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_s\\)\">\\(a_s\\)<\/span> are zero, the corner and fixed algebra are zero, and every assertion has precisely that interpretation. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For partial cocycles a,b, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n I(b,a)=\\{x\\in e_bMe_a:x a_s=b_s\\alpha_s(x)\\text{ for every }s\\in G\\}.\n \\tag{TIC14}\n\\]\">\\[\n I(b,a)=\\{x\\in e_bMe_a:x a_s=b_s\\alpha_s(x)\\text{ for every }s\\in G\\}.\n \\tag{TIC14}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe first index is the target. They are <strong>equivalent<\/strong>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\sim b\\)\">\\(a\\sim b\\)<\/span>, if a partial isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\in I(b,a)\\)\">\\(w\\in I(b,a)\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w^*w=e_a\\)\">\\(w^*w=e_a\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ww^*=e_b\\)\">\\(ww^*=e_b\\)<\/span>. They satisfy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\precsim b\\)\">\\(a\\precsim b\\)<\/span> if the same initial equality holds with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ww^*\\le e_b\\)\">\\(ww^*\\le e_b\\)<\/span>. These definitions include zero.<\/p>\n<p>The relation is the exact corner form of the two identities\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n a_s=w^*b_s\\alpha_s(w),\\qquad b_s=w a_s\\alpha_s(w^*).\n \\tag{TIC15}\n\\]\">\\[\n a_s=w^*b_s\\alpha_s(w),\\qquad b_s=w a_s\\alpha_s(w^*).\n \\tag{TIC15}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf an element c implements both identities before support compression, their identity-parameter equations are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c^*e_bc=e_a\\)\">\\(c^*e_bc=e_a\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c e_ac^*=e_b\\)\">\\(c e_ac^*=e_b\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w=e_bc e_a\\)\">\\(w=e_bc e_a\\)<\/span> has the indicated initial and final projections; compressing the parameter equations gives (TIC15). Conversely the two formulas follow from the intertwiner and support equations, so no choice outside the supports changes the definition. For subequivalence the final projection q is fixed and the second formula is for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b^q\\)\">\\(b^q\\)<\/span>. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto wxw^*\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto wxw^*\\)<\/span>, with inverse <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\mapsto w^*yw\\)\">\\(y\\mapsto w^*yw\\)<\/span>, is a whole normal equivariant isomorphism from the support action of a to the fixed reduction of the support action of b. The inherited whole positive averages and all bounded finite domains therefore transport through it.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-TIC.md","start_offset":10444,"end_offset":13787,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"e29fe202b916091ffacdd9ebdbcd9d229289b24d55c8174ece9b7120efed8266":{"markdown":"<a id=\"sar-4\"><\/a><a id=\"oa-flow.sar.crossed\"><\/a>\n## 4. The normal crossed product is recovered on its whole Hilbert space\n\nLet \\(\\alpha_n(f)(t)=f(t-n\\theta)\\) on \\(A_0=L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m)\\), and let\n\\(N=A_0\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\\) be the regular von Neumann crossed product. Write \\(\\pi\\) for its coefficient embedding and \\(s\\) for the unitary implementing \\(1\\in\\mathbb Z\\).\n\n**Theorem 4.** Under the trace hypothesis of Proposition 3, there is a unique normal isomorphism\n\\[\n \\Phi:N\\longrightarrow R,\\qquad\n \\Phi(\\pi(z))=v,\\quad \\Phi(s)=u.\n \\tag{SAR8}\n\\]\nIt carries the faithful normalized trace \\(m\\circ E\\) to \\(\\tau\\), where \\(E:N\\to A_0\\) is the regular expectation. The algebra \\(R\\) is a factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\).\n\n**Proof of the isomorphism.** The expectation \\(E\\) is faithful and normal by L128 FC1. Thus \\(\\sigma=m\\circ E\\) is a faithful normal state. If \\(a_k=E(xs^{-k})\\), the two square sums in FC1 and invariance of \\(m\\) give\n\\[\n \\sigma(x^*x)=\\sum_k\\int|a_k|^2\\,dm=\\sigma(xx^*).\n \\tag{SAR9}\n\\]\nEquality of these quadratic forms implies the trace identity by polarization. In detail, the Hermitian sesquilinear form\n\\(B(x,y)=\\sigma(y^*x)-\\sigma(xy^*)\\) has zero diagonal; evaluating it at \\(x+y\\) and \\(x+iy\\) gives \\(B(x,y)=0\\). Replace \\(y^*\\) by an arbitrary element. So \\(\\sigma\\) is a normalized trace on all of \\(N\\).\n\nBoth pairs \\((s,\\pi(z))\\) and \\((u,v)\\) satisfy the relation in (SAR5). In the respective tracial Hilbert spaces, the families\n\\[\n \\{\\Lambda_\\sigma(s^m\\pi(z)^n):(m,n)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\},\n \\qquad\n \\{\\Lambda_\\tau(u^m v^n):(m,n)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\}\n \\tag{SAR10}\n\\]\nare orthonormal. For the second family, multiply two monomials using (SAR5) and apply (SAR7). For the first, expectation kills every nonzero \\(s\\)-degree and integration kills every nonzero \\(z\\)-degree. When the two indices agree, the product is the identity. The same multiplication and adjoint relations hold in both polynomial algebras.\n\nEach family is total. In \\(R\\), the star algebra of the two generators has weak closure \\(R\\); BD's bounded density theorem and normality of \\(\\tau\\) make its vectors dense in \\(L^2(R,\\tau)\\). To spell out the last implication, bounded strong-star approximation \\(x_i\\to x\\) gives\n\\(\\tau((x_i-x)^*(x_i-x))\\to0\\), by the vector-series description of normal functionals. In \\(N\\), the normal coefficient map and \\(W^*(z)=A_0\\) show that \\(\\pi(z)\\) and \\(s\\) generate \\(N\\), so the same argument applies.\n\nConsequently the map carrying the first family in (SAR10) to the second extends to a unitary \\(W:L^2(N,\\sigma)\\to L^2(R,\\tau)\\). Polynomial multiplication shows that \\(W\\) intertwines left multiplication by \\(s,\\pi(z)\\) with left multiplication by \\(u,v\\), respectively.\n\nFor clarity, left multiplication of a finite von Neumann algebra \\(M\\) on \\(L^2(M,\\rho)\\), with faithful normal trace \\(\\rho\\), is a faithful normal representation. Boundedness follows from\n\\(\\rho(a^*x^*xa)\\le\\|x\\|^2\\rho(a^*a)\\), and faithfulness follows by applying an operator to \\(\\Lambda_\\rho(1)\\). Normality can be tested on increasing bounded positive nets: if \\(0\\le x_i\\uparrow x\\), then\n\\[\n \\|(x-x_i)\\Lambda_\\rho(a)\\|_2^2\n \\le \\|x\\|\\rho(a^*(x-x_i)a)\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SAR11}\n\\]\nDensity and the common operator bound give strong convergence on every vector. The normality equivalence is proved in [NF6](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.6), and ST2 gives a normal inverse onto the represented algebra.\n\nApply this to both tracial representations. Their von Neumann ranges are generated by the indicated left multipliers, so conjugation by \\(W\\), followed by the two faithful normal identifications, gives (SAR8). It is onto and its inverse is normal. It preserves the traces because \\(W\\) also carries the identity vector to the identity vector. Normal maps agreeing on the generators agree on the weakly dense polynomial algebra and then everywhere; this proves uniqueness.\n\n**Proof of the factor and type assertions.** On \\(L^2(R,\\tau)\\), conjugation by \\(u\\) and by \\(v\\) are unitaries. On the basis (SAR10) their eigenvalues are \\(q^n\\) and \\(q^{-m}\\). If \\(x\\in Z(R)\\), its vector is invariant under both. Orthogonality and irrationality force every coefficient except \\((0,0)\\) to vanish. Hence \\(\\Lambda_\\tau(x)=c\\Lambda_\\tau(1)\\); faithfulness gives \\(x=c1\\). Thus \\(R\\) is a factor.\n\nThe finite faithful trace excludes infinite projections: if a proper subprojection \\(p'<p\\) were equivalent to \\(p\\), traciality would give \\(\\tau(p-p')=0\\), contradicting faithfulness. Finally \\(A\\cong L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m)\\) is infinite dimensional, since it contains arbitrarily large families of nonzero orthogonal interval projections. Thus \\(R\\) is an infinite-dimensional finite factor. L128 TC3 proves that a finite factor with a minimal projection is a finite matrix algebra. Therefore \\(R\\) has no minimal projections and is type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\). \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>4. The normal crossed product is recovered on its whole Hilbert space<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_n(f)(t)=f(t-n\\theta)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_n(f)(t)=f(t-n\\theta)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_0=L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m)\\)\">\\(A_0=L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m)\\)<\/span>, and let\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=A_0\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(N=A_0\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> be the regular von Neumann crossed product. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> for its coefficient embedding and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> for the unitary implementing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\in\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(1\\in\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 4.<\/strong> Under the trace hypothesis of Proposition 3, there is a unique normal isomorphism\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Phi:N\\longrightarrow R,\\qquad\n \\Phi(\\pi(z))=v,\\quad \\Phi(s)=u.\n \\tag{SAR8}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Phi:N\\longrightarrow R,\\qquad\n \\Phi(\\pi(z))=v,\\quad \\Phi(s)=u.\n \\tag{SAR8}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt carries the faithful normalized trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\circ E\\)\">\\(m\\circ E\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E:N\\to A_0\\)\">\\(E:N\\to A_0\\)<\/span> is the regular expectation. The algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span> is a factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof of the isomorphism.<\/strong> The expectation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> is faithful and normal by L128 FC1. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma=m\\circ E\\)\">\\(\\sigma=m\\circ E\\)<\/span> is a faithful normal state. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_k=E(xs^{-k})\\)\">\\(a_k=E(xs^{-k})\\)<\/span>, the two square sums in FC1 and invariance of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span> give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sigma(x^*x)=\\sum_k\\int|a_k|^2\\,dm=\\sigma(xx^*).\n \\tag{SAR9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sigma(x^*x)=\\sum_k\\int|a_k|^2\\,dm=\\sigma(xx^*).\n \\tag{SAR9}\n\\]<\/span>\nEquality of these quadratic forms implies the trace identity by polarization. In detail, the Hermitian sesquilinear form\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(x,y)=\\sigma(y^*x)-\\sigma(xy^*)\\)\">\\(B(x,y)=\\sigma(y^*x)-\\sigma(xy^*)\\)<\/span> has zero diagonal; evaluating it at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x+y\\)\">\\(x+y\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x+iy\\)\">\\(x+iy\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(x,y)=0\\)\">\\(B(x,y)=0\\)<\/span>. Replace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y^*\\)\">\\(y^*\\)<\/span> by an arbitrary element. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span> is a normalized trace on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Both pairs <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((s,\\pi(z))\\)\">\\((s,\\pi(z))\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((u,v)\\)\">\\((u,v)\\)<\/span> satisfy the relation in (SAR5). In the respective tracial Hilbert spaces, the families\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\{\\Lambda_\\sigma(s^m\\pi(z)^n):(m,n)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\},\n \\qquad\n \\{\\Lambda_\\tau(u^m v^n):(m,n)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\}\n \\tag{SAR10}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\{\\Lambda_\\sigma(s^m\\pi(z)^n):(m,n)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\},\n \\qquad\n \\{\\Lambda_\\tau(u^m v^n):(m,n)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\}\n \\tag{SAR10}\n\\]<\/span>\nare orthonormal. For the second family, multiply two monomials using (SAR5) and apply (SAR7). For the first, expectation kills every nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>-degree and integration kills every nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>-degree. When the two indices agree, the product is the identity. The same multiplication and adjoint relations hold in both polynomial algebras.<\/p>\n<p>Each family is total. In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span>, the star algebra of the two generators has weak closure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span>; BD's bounded density theorem and normality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span> make its vectors dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(R,\\tau)\\)\">\\(L^2(R,\\tau)\\)<\/span>. To spell out the last implication, bounded strong-star approximation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_i\\to x\\)\">\\(x_i\\to x\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau((x_i-x)^*(x_i-x))\\to0\\)\">\\(\\tau((x_i-x)^*(x_i-x))\\to0\\)<\/span>, by the vector-series description of normal functionals. In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, the normal coefficient map and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W^*(z)=A_0\\)\">\\(W^*(z)=A_0\\)<\/span> show that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(z)\\)\">\\(\\pi(z)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> generate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, so the same argument applies.<\/p>\n<p>Consequently the map carrying the first family in (SAR10) to the second extends to a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W:L^2(N,\\sigma)\\to L^2(R,\\tau)\\)\">\\(W:L^2(N,\\sigma)\\to L^2(R,\\tau)\\)<\/span>. Polynomial multiplication shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> intertwines left multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s,\\pi(z)\\)\">\\(s,\\pi(z)\\)<\/span> with left multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u,v\\)\">\\(u,v\\)<\/span>, respectively.<\/p>\n<p>For clarity, left multiplication of a finite von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(M,\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^2(M,\\rho)\\)<\/span>, with faithful normal trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span>, is a faithful normal representation. Boundedness follows from\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(a^*x^*xa)\\le\\|x\\|^2\\rho(a^*a)\\)\">\\(\\rho(a^*x^*xa)\\le\\|x\\|^2\\rho(a^*a)\\)<\/span>, and faithfulness follows by applying an operator to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\rho(1)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\rho(1)\\)<\/span>. Normality can be tested on increasing bounded positive nets: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\le x_i\\uparrow x\\)\">\\(0\\le x_i\\uparrow x\\)<\/span>, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|(x-x_i)\\Lambda_\\rho(a)\\|_2^2\n \\le \\|x\\|\\rho(a^*(x-x_i)a)\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SAR11}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|(x-x_i)\\Lambda_\\rho(a)\\|_2^2\n \\le \\|x\\|\\rho(a^*(x-x_i)a)\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SAR11}\n\\]<\/span>\nDensity and the common operator bound give strong convergence on every vector. The normality equivalence is proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.6\">NF6<\/a>, and ST2 gives a normal inverse onto the represented algebra.<\/p>\n<p>Apply this to both tracial representations. Their von Neumann ranges are generated by the indicated left multipliers, so conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span>, followed by the two faithful normal identifications, gives (SAR8). It is onto and its inverse is normal. It preserves the traces because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> also carries the identity vector to the identity vector. Normal maps agreeing on the generators agree on the weakly dense polynomial algebra and then everywhere; this proves uniqueness.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof of the factor and type assertions.<\/strong> On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(R,\\tau)\\)\">\\(L^2(R,\\tau)\\)<\/span>, conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> and by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> are unitaries. On the basis (SAR10) their eigenvalues are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q^n\\)\">\\(q^n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q^{-m}\\)\">\\(q^{-m}\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in Z(R)\\)\">\\(x\\in Z(R)\\)<\/span>, its vector is invariant under both. Orthogonality and irrationality force every coefficient except <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,0)\\)\">\\((0,0)\\)<\/span> to vanish. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\tau(x)=c\\Lambda_\\tau(1)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\tau(x)=c\\Lambda_\\tau(1)\\)<\/span>; faithfulness gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=c1\\)\">\\(x=c1\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span> is a factor.<\/p>\n<p>The finite faithful trace excludes infinite projections: if a proper subprojection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;&lt;p\\)\">\\(p&#x27;&lt;p\\)<\/span> were equivalent to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, traciality would give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau(p-p&#x27;)=0\\)\">\\(\\tau(p-p&#x27;)=0\\)<\/span>, contradicting faithfulness. Finally <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\cong L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m)\\)\">\\(A\\cong L^\\infty(\\mathbb T,m)\\)<\/span> is infinite dimensional, since it contains arbitrarily large families of nonzero orthogonal interval projections. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span> is an infinite-dimensional finite factor. L128 TC3 proves that a finite factor with a minimal projection is a finite matrix algebra. Therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span> has no minimal projections and is type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-SAR.md","start_offset":9480,"end_offset":14374,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
